<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\title[Attracting invariant graphs and porcupine attractors]{Attracting invariant graphs and higher dimensional porcupine attractors}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\author[Zaj and Ghane]{M‎. ‎Zaj  and F‎. ‎H‎. ‎Ghane}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\address[Zaj and Ghane]{Department Mathematics‎, ‎Ferdowsi University of Mashhad}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\affil[1]{Department of Computer Science‎, ‎\LaTeX\ University}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\affil[2]{Department of Mechanical Engineering‎, ‎\LaTeX\ %University}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\keywords{skew products‎, ‎maximal attractor‎, ‎synchronization‎, ‎invariant graph‎, ‎entropy.} \subjclass[2010]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎{37A05; 37C40; 37C70; 37H15; 37D25; 37D30.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{document}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\maketitle‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\maketitle‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{abstract}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We study discrete time skew product dynamical systems with higher dimensional fibers and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎non-uniformly contracting fiber dynamics‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For an open class of such systems driven by expanding or solenoidal base maps‎, ‎we establish a‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎sharp geometric-statistical dichotomy for maximal attractors‎: ‎either the attractor is a‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎continuous invariant graph‎, ‎or it is a \emph{solenoidal $n$-porcupine attractor} consisting of‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎a measurable invariant graph together with uncountably many embedded $C^{1}$ spines‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In the porcupine regime we identify a symbolic branching mechanism inside the blending region‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎that generates topological transitivity and positive topological entropy on the maximal‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎attractor‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This provides a rigorous source of chaotic dynamics without assuming uniform hyperbolicity or‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎the existence of Markov partitions‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎To describe statistical behaviour under weak and non-uniform contraction‎, ‎we introduce the‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎notion of \emph{fiberwise SRB-like measures}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Under natural assumptions‎, ‎these measures exist uniquely‎, ‎are ergodic‎, ‎and attract fiber‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎distributions at an exponential rate‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎As a consequence‎, ‎we obtain a robust form of \emph{statistical synchronization}‎, ‎whereby‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎typical fiber distributions converge exponentially to a common invariant probability measure‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎despite the geometric complexity of the attractor‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Finally‎, ‎we establish regularity criteria for invariant graphs‎, ‎prove $C^{2}$ persistence and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎parameter stability of the geometric and statistical dichotomy‎, ‎and present a detailed example‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎verifying all hypotheses and illustrating the porcupine geometry‎, ‎chaotic dynamics‎, ‎and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎statistical synchronization‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{abstract}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\maketitle‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\thispagestyle{empty}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\tableofcontents‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\section{Introduction}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Attractors in forced‎, ‎random‎, ‎or nonautonomous dynamical systems exhibit a wide range of‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎topological‎, ‎geometric‎, ‎and statistical behaviours depending on the perspective adopted‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎including topological attractors‎, ‎invariant graph attractors‎, ‎pullback attractors‎, ‎and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎SRB--measure attractors‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\cite{robinson1998dynamical,magal2005global}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A unifying and flexible framework for studying such phenomena is provided by discrete time‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎skew products of the form‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{eq:skew}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎F(\theta,y)=(S(\theta),f_\theta(y)),\qquad‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎F:\Theta\times Y\to\Theta\times Y‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where $(\Theta,S)$ is a compact driving system and each map‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$f_\theta:Y\to Y$ acts on a compact fiber space $Y$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Skew products arise naturally in deterministic forcing‎, ‎random and stochastic dynamics‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎partially hyperbolic extensions‎, ‎and time dependent control systems‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎They capture the interaction between expansion or complexity in the base dynamics and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎contraction‎, ‎neutrality‎, ‎or non-uniform behaviour in the fibers‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This interaction is responsible for many of the non-hyperbolic mechanisms observed in modern‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎applied and theoretical dynamics‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Skew products with weak or non-uniform contraction in the fiber direction arise broadly in‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎applications‎:</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{itemize}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \emph{Neuroscience and coupled oscillators.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Forced neuron models and slow-fast synaptic systems often combine expanding phase dynamics‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎with multidimensional internal states subject to weak contraction‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Complex firing patterns and intermittent synchronization naturally correspond to nontrivial‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎fiber attractors‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \emph{Climate and geophysical models.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Quasiperiodically or randomly driven energy--balance and climate models involve multidimensional‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎internal variables with non-uniform contraction‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fiberwise statistical laws describe averaged climate responses‎, ‎while synchronization reflects‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎alignment of internal climate modes‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \emph{Control systems and network dynamics.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Hybrid controllers‎, ‎symbolic switching protocols‎, ‎and time dependent routing schemes often‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎exhibit expanding base actions coupled with weakly contractive internal subsystems‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Understanding long term behaviour under irregular forcing is essential for robustness and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎stability‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \emph{Random dynamical systems and filtering.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Nonlinear filtering and stochastic forcing frequently induce contraction of internal states‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎leading to collapse of distributions toward invariant statistical laws‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A rigorous description of this convergence requires a fiberwise statistical framework‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{itemize}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎These applications motivate the need for a unified theory of skew products with‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎higher dimensional fibers and non-uniform contraction‎, ‎capable of describing both their‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\emph{geometric structure} (graphs‎, ‎multi graphs‎, ‎porcupine type attractors) and their‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\emph{statistical behaviour} (SRB-like measures and synchronization phenomena)‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\subsection*{Classical theory and limitations}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎When the fiber dynamics is \emph{uniformly contracting}‎, ‎classical results guarantee the‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎existence of a unique continuous invariant graph attracting all fibers; see Stark‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\cite{stark1999regularity}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In the case of one-dimensional fibers‎, ‎finer geometric classifications have been obtained‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎including pinched attractors‎, ‎fat solenoids‎, ‎and bony attractors‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\cite{jager2003quasiperiodically,tsujii2001fat,kudryashov2010bony}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎From a statistical perspective‎, ‎the foundational work of Alves--Bonatti--Viana‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\cite{alves2000srb}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎established the existence of SRB measures for partially hyperbolic systems whose central‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎direction is mostly expanding‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This theory explains how physical measures persist under strong non-uniformity‎, ‎but it is‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎intrinsically expansion based and does not address statistical behaviour arising from‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\emph{fiberwise contraction}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Despite these advances‎, ‎major challenges remain for forced systems and general skew products‎:</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{itemize}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item most existing results either assume $\dim Y=1$ or rely on strong uniform hyperbolicity;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item non-uniform contraction destroys classical graph regularity and produces geometric‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎structures‎, ‎such as multi graphs and porcupines‎, ‎with no one--dimensional analogue;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item higher dimensional fibers lack the order and monotonicity tools available in dimension‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎one;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item existing SRB theories do not describe statistical behaviour in weakly contracting fiber‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎regimes nor explain synchronization in this setting;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item a unified geometric and statistical classification of maximal attractors for nonlinear skew‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎products has been lacking‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{itemize}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\subsection*{Recent developments and remaining gaps}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Several recent works address parts of this picture‎, ‎though none provides a complete‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎geometric statistical theory‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\medskip‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\noindent\textbf{(A) Non-uniform geometry and multi--graphs.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Rabiee--Ghane--Zaj~\cite{rabiee2022open} construct multi graph and bony multi graph attractors‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎but only for one dimensional fibers and without statistical results or synchronization‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎phenomena‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\noindent\textbf{(B) Non-hyperbolic behaviour in dimension one.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Barrientos--Cisneros~\cite{barrientos2024zero} and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Barrientos--Chávez~\cite{barrientos2024historical} demonstrate robust zero Lyapunov exponents‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎and historical behaviour in transitive skew products‎, ‎again restricted to one dimensional‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎fibers‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\noindent\textbf{(C) Nonautonomous and fractional order systems.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Cui--Kloeden~\cite{cui2024skew} establish existence of bounded skew product attractors for‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎fractional equations‎, ‎but do not address nonlinear geometric classification or SRB type‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎statistics‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\noindent\textbf{(D) Invariant distributions and fractal graphs.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Xu--Zhang~\cite{xu2024invariant} study invariant distributions and fractal graph structures‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎without considering attractor classification or fiberwise statistics‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\noindent\textbf{(E) Empirical and variational approaches.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Díaz et al.~\cite{diaz2022variational} and Crovisier et al.~\cite{crovisier2020empirical} provide‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎deep insights into ergodic measures and empirical distributions under weak hyperbolicity‎, ‎but‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎not specifically for skew products with heterogeneous forcing‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\noindent\textbf{(F) SIAM-line advances in synchronization and linear theory.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Recent SIAM contributions‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\cite{jardon2024persistent,beyn2023angular,beyn2022angular}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎develop synchronization criteria and angular-value theory for nonautonomous linear systems‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎These tools do not address nonlinear geometric dichotomies or statistical synchronization in‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎skew products‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\subsection*{Contributions of the present work}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This paper develops a unified geometric and statistical theory for skew products with‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{itemize}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item fibers of dimension $\dim Y\ge2$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item non-uniform weak contraction in the fiber direction‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item expanding or solenoidal base dynamics‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{itemize}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Our main contributions are as follows‎:</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \textbf{Geometric dichotomy for maximal attractors.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We prove a sharp dichotomy‎: ‎for an open class of skew products‎, ‎the maximal attractor is either‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎(a) a continuous invariant graph‎, ‎or‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎(b) a \emph{solenoidal $n$--porcupine attractor} consisting of a measurable invariant graph‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎together with uncountably many embedded $C^1$ spines‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This provides the first such classification for higher dimensional‎, ‎non-uniformly contracting‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎fibers‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \textbf{Chaotic dynamics inside porcupine attractors.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In the porcupine regime we prove the existence of symbolic branching mechanisms that generate‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎topological transitivity and positive topological entropy on the maximal attractor‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This establishes the presence of chaotic dynamics without assuming uniform hyperbolicity or‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Markov partitions‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \textbf{Fiberwise SRB-like measures.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We introduce the notion of \emph{fiberwise SRB-like measures} describing statistical behaviour‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎inside weakly contracting fibers‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We prove existence‎, ‎uniqueness‎, ‎ergodicity‎, ‎and exponential convergence‎, ‎extending the SRB‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎program of \cite{alves2000srb} to contraction dominated skew products‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \textbf{Statistical synchronization.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Despite the geometric complexity of porcupine attractors‎, ‎fiber distributions converge‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎exponentially to the unique fiberwise SRB-like measure‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This phenomenon‎, ‎which we term \emph{statistical synchronization}‎, ‎has no analogue in‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎one dimensional geometric studies and no counterpart in expansion based SRB theory‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item \textbf{Regularity‎, ‎robustness‎, ‎and examples.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We establish regularity criteria for invariant graphs‎, ‎analyze the breakdown of regularity‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎under loss of uniform contraction‎, ‎prove $C^2$ persistence of the dichotomy‎, ‎and provide a‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎detailed example verifying all hypotheses‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\subsection*{Organization of the paper}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Section \ref{s2} develops tools for random iterations of weakly contractive maps‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Section \ref{wbr} constructs blending regions and covering mechanisms‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Section \ref{sec3} proves the geometric dichotomy‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Section \ref{secchaotic} establishes chaotic dynamics in the porcupine regime‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Sections \ref{Statistical} and \ref{secexample} develop symbolic separation and entropy estimates‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Section~8 constructs fiberwise SRB-like measures and proves statistical synchronization‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Section~9 presents a detailed example illustrating the theory‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Attractors and Skew Products}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We start recalling some classical definitions related to attractors‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Many variants for the definition of an attractor can be found in the literature‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎see \cite{milnor1985concept} for a discussion‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎let  $M$  be a compact‎, ‎connected‎, ‎closed‎, ‎and smooth Riemannian manifold‎,  ‎$m$ the normalized Lebesgue measure on $M$‎, ‎and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎$\psi \colon M \to M$ a continuous map‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎Given a closed $\psi$-invariant set $A$‎,  ‎its \emph{basin of attraction} $B(A)$ is the set of points   whose future orbits tend  (in the Hausdorff metric) to $A$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎We say that $A$ is  \emph{Milnor attractor} for $\psi$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎if $m(B(A))&gt;0$ \cite{milnor1985concept}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In some definitions of attractors‎, ‎the basin of attraction $B(A)$ is an open set‎. ‎However‎, ‎here we only‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎require a basin of attraction with positive measure‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A compact set $D$ is called \emph{trapping} for $\psi$ if $\psi(D) \subset \textnormal{int}(D)$‎, ‎where $\textnormal{int}(D)$ denotes the interior of $D$‎. ‎In such a case‎,  ‎the‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎closed $\psi$-invariant set‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{e18}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A_{max}(D,\psi):= \bigcap_{n\geq 0}\psi^n(D)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is  the \emph{maximal attractor} for $\psi$ in $D$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In discrete time‎, ‎random dynamical systems influenced by a deterministic external factor are modeled as skew product systems‎. </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎Consider compact metric spaces $\Theta$ and $Y$ and a continuous map‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$S \colon \Theta \to \Theta$‎. ‎Fixed $S$‎, ‎we denote by $\mathcal{C}_{S‎, ‎\mathrm{Lip}} (\Theta\times Y)$ the set of skew products‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\label{e.asin}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎F=F_{(S,f_{\theta})} \colon \Theta \times Y \to \Theta \times Y‎, ‎\qquad‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎F(\theta,y) =(S(\theta)‎, ‎f_\theta(y))‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎whose fiber maps $f_\theta$‎, ‎$\theta \in \Theta$‎, ‎are Lipschitz and continuously depend on $\theta$‎. ‎Here‎, ‎$S$ denotes the {\em{base transformation}}‎ </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎and $f_\theta$ represents the {\em{fiber transformation}} (or {\em{forced system}})‎. </p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎The fiber map $f_\theta$ can be considered as a map‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎from $Y$ to $ Y$‎, ‎where $ Y$ is the fiber space‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We adopt the usual notation $ F^{n}(\theta,y ) = (S^{n}\theta‎ , ‎f_{\theta}^{n}(y))$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for the iterates $F^{n}$ of $F$‎, ‎where $f^{n}_{\theta} = f_{S^{n-1}(\theta)} \circ \cdots \circ f_{\theta}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Hence‎, ‎$f_\theta^{n+k}(y) = f_{S^k \theta}^n (f^k_\theta(y))$‎. ‎If the fiber maps are invertible‎, ‎for $n = 1$ and $k =-1$ this includes the identity and $f_\theta^{-1}(y) = (f_{S^{-1}\theta})^{-1}(y)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Invariant graphs}\label{assp}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We now discuss invariant graphs and their variations‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎In the study of skew products‎, ‎invariant graphs‎, ‎particularly attracting invariant graphs‎, ‎play an essential role‎. ‎In fact‎, ‎they are the natural substitutes of fixed points and considerably simplify the dynamics of the forced systems‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}‎    </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $m$ be a $S$-invariant measure‎. ‎A measurable function $\gamma:\Theta \to Y$ is‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎         </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item[i)]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎an {\em{$F$-invariant graph}} if for every $\theta \in \Theta$ it holds‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎F(\theta,\gamma(\theta))=(S(\theta),\gamma(S(\theta)))‎, ‎\quad \text{or} \quad f_\theta(\gamma(\theta))=\gamma(S(\theta))‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item[ii)]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎an {\em{$(F,m)$-invariant graph}} if‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{1graph}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎f_\theta(\gamma(\theta))=\gamma(S(\theta))‎,  ‎\quad \text{for} \ m-a.e‎. ‎\ \theta\in \Theta‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎With slight abuse of notation‎, ‎we call the point set‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\Gamma=\Gamma_\gamma‎ :‎=\{(\theta,\gamma(\theta)):\theta \in \Theta\}‎ </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$F$ or $(F,m)$-invariant graph as well‎, ‎but labeled with‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎the corresponding capital letter‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The graph $\Gamma$ is an attracting invariant graph if for $m$-a.e‎. ‎$\theta \in \Theta$‎, ‎one has that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\lim_{n \to \infty}|\pi_y(F^n(\theta,y))-\gamma(S^n(\theta))|=0‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for all $y \in Y$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In skew products‎, ‎Lyapunov exponents  are  useful quantitative indicators of the asymptotic expansion and contraction rates (along  fibers)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}[Maximal Lyapunov exponent]\label{max}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $F‎ :‎\Theta \times Y \to \Theta \times Y$  be a skew product whose fiber maps $f_\theta$ are differentiable‎. ‎The {\em{maximal Lyapunov exponent of an $F$-invariant probability measure $\mu$}} is defined by‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{lya1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\lambda(\mu,F):=\lim_{n \to \infty}\frac{1}{n}\int_{\Theta \times Y} \log \|Df_\theta^{n}(x)\| \‎, ‎d\mu(\theta,x)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The {\em{maximal Lyapunov exponent of an $(F‎ ,‎m)$-invariant graph $\gamma$}} is defined by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{lya}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\lambda_m(\gamma):=\int_{\Theta} \log \|Df_\theta(\gamma(\theta))\|\,dm(\theta)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Partial hyperbolicity}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider a smooth manifold $M$ (maybe noncompact)‎, ‎a differentiable dynamical system $f:M \to M$ and an $f$-invariant set $\Lambda$‎. ‎A splitting $T_{\Lambda}M=E\oplus F$ is \emph{dominated} if $E$ and $F$ are $Df$-invariant and there are constants $m&gt;0$ and $K&lt; 1$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{dom}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\|(D_xf^m) |_{E_x}\| \|((D_xf^m)^{-1}) |_{F_{f^m(x)}}\|&lt;K‎, ‎\ \text{for \ all} \ x\in \Lambda‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A $Df$-invariant bundle $E$ defined on $\Lambda$ is uniformly contracting (resp‎. ‎expanding) if there are $C&gt;0$ and $0&lt; \lambda&lt;1$ such that for every $n&gt;0$ one has‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{ce}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\|D_xf^n(v)\| \leq C\lambda^n \|v\| \ \ (\text{resp.} \ \|D_xf^{-n}(v)\| \leq C\lambda^n \|v\|) \ \text{for \ all} \ x\in \Lambda \ \text{and} \ v \in E‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We say that the set $\Lambda$ is \emph{partially hyperbolic} if there is a dominated splitting‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$E\oplus F$ of $T_{\Lambda}M$ such that either $E$ is uniformly contracting or $F$ is uniformly expanding‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In the first case we write $T_{\Lambda}M=E^{ss}\oplus E^{cu}$‎, ‎otherwise we write $T_{\Lambda}M=E^{uu}\oplus E^{cs}$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎An $f$-invariant set $\Lambda$ is \emph{strongly partially hyperbolic} if there is a $df$-invariant dominated splitting‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$E^s \oplus E^c \oplus E^u$ where $df_{|E^s}$ is uniformly contracting‎, ‎$df_{|E^u}$ is uniformly‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎expanding‎, ‎$E^c$ is non-trivial and non-hyperbolic‎, ‎and the three bundles $E^s$‎, ‎$E^c$‎, ‎and $E^u$ are non-trivial‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Main results}\label{mm}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The space of all skew products $F_{(\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎as in \eqref{skewT1} is denoted by $\mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We denote by $\mathcal{C} (\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎the space of skew product maps $\mathbb{F}= \mathbb{F}_{F}$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎which are an extension of some‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$F\in \mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We equip these spaces with the metric given by \eqref{e113}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For the skew product with trapping region $D$‎, ‎the set $\Lambda:=A_{max}(D)$ is the maximal attractor‎. </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given any point $a$ in the base space let $\Lambda_a:=\Lambda \cap \{a\}\times \mathbb{B}_n$‎. ‎We call $\Lambda_a$ a \emph{spine} and say that it is \emph{non-trivial} if it is not a singleton‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that for any $a$ in the base space‎, ‎$\Lambda=\bigcup_{}\Lambda_a$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\begin{remark}\label{mfi}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Note that the baker map $\Phi_\ell$ preserves Lebesgue measure‎, ‎denoted by %$\nu_{\Phi_\ell}$, and is ergodic \cite{devaney1981baker}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The following concept is a generalization of porcupine horseshoes discovered by Diaz and Gelfert in \cite{diaz2010porcupine}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Now‎, ‎we define Solenoidal porcupine-like graph as follows‎:</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}[Solenoidal porcupine-like graph]\label{sol}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given $\mathbb{F} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎a compact $\mathbb{F}$-invariant set $\Lambda$ is‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎a {\em{solenoidal $n$-porcupine-like graph}} if‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item [(1)] $\Lambda$ is the maximal attractor for $\mathbb{F}$ in some trapping region $D$;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item [(2)]   there exist a subset $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}_\ell$ of $\mathcal{S}_\ell$ with‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎$\nu_\ell(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}_\ell)=1$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎and an $(\mathbb{F},\nu_\ell)$-invariant (attracting) graph $\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎defined on   $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}_\ell$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">     ‎such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎$\Lambda$ is the union of  the graph $\Gamma_{\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}}$  and a set‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎consisting‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎of uncountably many one dimensional $C^1$ closed submanifold‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎contained in the closure of $\Gamma_{\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}}$ (called {\em{nontrivial spines}})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The map $\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$ is called  the {\em{graph function of the maximal attractor.}}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{figure}[ht]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\centering‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">% --- ‎Left box‎: ‎invariant graph‎ ---</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[thick] (-4.8,-1.8) rectangle (-0.3,2);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\node at (-2.55,2.25) {\textbf{(A) Invariant Graph}};‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">% ‎axes‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[-&gt;] (-4.5,-1.6)‎ -- ‎(-4.5,1.8);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[-&gt;] (-4.6,-1.5)‎ -- ‎(-0.5,-1.5);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\node at (-4.7,1.6) ;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\node at (-2.5,-1.75);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">% ‎smooth invariant graph‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[ultra thick,blue!60]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎(-4.5,-0.3)‎ .. ‎controls (-3.3,1.2)‎ .. ‎(-2.3,0.8)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">           .. ‎controls (-1.5,0.5)‎ .. ‎(-0.5,0.2);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">% --- ‎Right box‎: ‎porcupine attractor‎ ---</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[thick] (0.3,-1.8) rectangle (4.8,2);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\node at (2.55,2.25) {\textbf{(B) Porcupine Attractor}};‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">% ‎axes‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[-&gt;] (0.6,-1.6)‎ -- ‎(0.6,1.8);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[-&gt;] (0.5,-1.5)‎ -- ‎(4.5,-1.5);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\node at (0.4,1.6) ;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\node at (2.5,-1.75) ;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">% ‎measurable backbone graph‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\draw[ultra thick,blue!60]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎(0.6,-0.1)‎ .. ‎controls (1.6,0.4)‎ .. ‎(2.6,0.25)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">          .. ‎controls (3.4,0.1)‎ .. ‎(4.3,0.0);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">% ‎deterministic spines (no randomness)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">‎\foreach \x/\h in {0.6/1.4,1.1/1.0,1.6/1.5,2.1/1.2,2.6/1.7,3.1/1.3,3.6/1.6,4.1/1.1}‎ {</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">    ‎\draw[red!70!black,thick] (\x,0.0)‎ -- ‎(\x,\h);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:8;">}</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{tikzpicture}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\caption{‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Schematic representation of the geometric dichotomy for maximal attractors in skew‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎products with non-uniformly contracting fibers‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In the absence of a continuous invariant graph‎, ‎the maximal attractor exhibits a‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\emph{solenoidal porcupine structure}‎, ‎consisting of a measurable invariant graph‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎(the central core) decorated by uncountably many embedded $C^{1}$ spines‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Each spine arises from stable remnants of random fiber compositions and varies‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎measurably with the base point‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The figure illustrates how expansion in the base‎, ‎combined with weak contraction‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎and parabolic behavior in the fibers‎, ‎destroys global graph regularity while preserving‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎a compact attracting set‎, ‎as described in Theorem \ref{thm1}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">}</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{fig:dichotomy}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{figure}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Here we have three main results as follows‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item [$\blacktriangleright$] Let $f$ be a $C^2$ weakly contractive injective map and $\mathcal{N}_f$ be a normally hyperbolic adapted $C^2$ neighborhood of $f$ as in Proposition \ref{open}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then for every sequence $\mathbf{t}=(\cdots‎, ‎t_{-n}‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0)$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎with values in $I$‎, ‎such that for each $n$‎, ‎$f_{t_{-n}} \in \mathcal{N}_f$ or $f_{t_{-n}}$ is a $C^2$ injective map with $f_{t_{-n}}(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset \text{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎the intersection‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}=    \bigcap_{k\ge 0}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{t}} (k)‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎\qquad‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{t}} (k)‎ :‎=       f^k_{\mathbf{t}} (\mathbb{B}_{n})‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">      ‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is either a single point or a one dimensional $C^1$ closed submanifold‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item [$\blacktriangleright$] There is an open neighbourhood‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎$ \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ in‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎$\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎such that every $\mathbb{F}\in \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ has a maximal attractor $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is  topologically transitive and satisfies the following dichotomy‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎\item [$-$] either $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ is an attracting continuous invariant graph;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎\item [$-$] or $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ is a solenoidal $n$-porcupine-like graph‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">      ‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Note that the baker map $\Phi_\ell$ preserves Lebesgue measure denoted by $\nu_{\Phi_\ell}$ (see Remark \ref{mfi})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%Assume $G \in \mathcal{B}_{\Phi_\ell}(\mathbb{T}^2 \times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎. ‎We denote by $\mathcal{M}_{\nu_{\Phi_\ell}}(G)$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎%the space of all $G$-invariant probability measures $\mu_{\Phi_\ell}$ on $\mathbb{T}^2 \times \mathbb{B}_n$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%with marginal $\nu_{\Phi_\ell}$‎, ‎that is the set of all $G$-invariant measures which project to $\nu_{\Phi_\ell}$ in the first coordinate‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item [$\blacktriangleright$] For a weakly attracting pair $(f_1,\ldots,f_k)$  (as defined in definition \ref{weakpair}) with blending‎ </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎region $B$‎, ‎and that the base map $T\colon M\to M$ preserves an ergodic‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎absolutely continuous probability $\nu$‎,  ‎the skew product‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$F(a,y)=(T(a),f_a(y))$ admits a fiberwise SRB-like measure‎. ‎In addition this fiberwise SRB-like measure is unique and ergodic‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\begin{theorem 1}\label{t1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%There are $\ell\in \mathbb{N}$ and an open set $\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{V}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^2}$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%in $\mathcal{B}_{\Phi_\ell}(\mathbb{T}^2 \times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%such that for every $G$ in $\mathcal{V}$ the maximal attractor $A_{max}(G):=A_{max}(\mathbb{T}^2 \times \mathbb{B}_n)$  is either a continuous attracting invariant graph or a thick $n$-porcupine attractor‎. ‎In particular‎, ‎the set of skew products $G \in \mathcal{V}$ posses $n$-porcupine attractor is nonempty‎. </p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎%\end{theorem 1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Main ingredients and organization}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We will provide an overview of the paper's contents and briefly outline the main ideas used to prove the theorem‎. ‎In Section \ref{wbr}‎, ‎we introduce two local tools‎, ‎namely the weakly attracting pair and the weakly blending region of $\mathbb{R}^n$‎, ‎which allow us to construct $n$-porcupine attractors‎. ‎To construct the weakly blending regions of $\mathbb{R}^n$‎, ‎we consider contractive maps with non-uniform contraction rates‎, ‎also known as weakly contractive maps‎. ‎For a specific class of weakly contractive maps‎, ‎the approach presented in \cite{alves2000srb} yields a bounded distortion property for random compositions of fiber maps‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Partial hyperbolicity is a crucial element in our approach‎. ‎Furthermore‎, ‎we demonstrate that under small perturbations of a weakly contractive map‎, ‎the forward random iterations of the ball $\mathbb{B}_n \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ are either a single point or a $C^1$-submanifold (as shown in Proposition \ref{open})‎. ‎Based on these findings‎, ‎we investigate the existence of a weakly blending region for each compact ball $\mathbb{B}_n \subset \mathbb{R}^n$‎, ‎as detailed in Theorem \ref{problend}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Section \ref{s.attractinggraphs} is dedicated to studying skew products that have non-uniform contraction rates along the fibers‎. ‎This particular condition enables us to utilize‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\cite[Thm‎. ‎1.4]{stark1999regularity} to establish attracting invariant graphs‎. ‎In Section \ref{sec3}‎, ‎we complete the proof of Theorem 1 and introduce an open set $\mathcal{V}$ that meets the requirements outlined in Theorem 1‎. ‎The maps within $\mathcal{V}$ have average fiber contraction rates‎, ‎and their fiber maps exhibit a weakly blending region‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎These are the primary ingredients involved in constructing $n$-porcupine attractors‎. </p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\section{Random iterations of maps}\label{s2}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this section‎, ‎we consider contractive maps with non-uniform contraction rates‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Following \cite{alber1997principle}‎, ‎given a metric space $(X,d)$ we say that a continuous map $f\colon X \to X$ is \emph{weakly contractive} if $d(f(x),f(y)) &lt; d(x,y)$ for every $x,y \in X$ with $x\neq y$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎It is well-known‎, ‎see \cite[Coro.~3]{jager2003quasiperiodically} and \cite{bielecki1995iterated}‎, ‎that if $f$ is weakly contractive and $(X,d)$ is compact then $f$ has a unique fixed point $x \in X$ which is attracting in the sense that $\lim_{k \to \infty}f^k(y)=x$‎, ‎for every $y \in X$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Here‎, ‎we take $X=\mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎where $\mathbb{B}_n$ denotes the closed unit ball of $\mathbb{R}^n$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎for a weakly contractive map $f \colon \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎we can speak of its \emph{attracting fixed point}‎, ‎denoted by $x_f$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A key ingredient in our construction of weak blending regions is the following‎:</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}[Parabolic attracting fixed point]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $f\colon \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{B}_n$ be a smooth weakly contractive map‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The attracting fixed point $x_f$ of $f$ is \emph{parabolic} if the Jacobian matrix $Df(x_f)$ possesses $1$ as a simple eigenvalue and the other eigenvalues have absolute values less than $1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The parabolic fixed point $x_f$ is \emph{generic} whenever the eigenvalues of $Df(x_f)$ are all positive and of multiplicity one‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎we denote by $\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$ the set of $C^2$ weakly contractive injective maps $f\colon \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{B}_n$ with $f(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset \text{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$ and whose attracting fixed point $x_f$ is generic and parabolic‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that $x_f$ belongs to the interior $\text{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$ of $\mathbb{B}_n$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎There is a basis of neighborhoods $V_{n,f}$ of $x_f$ with $f(\overline{V_{n,f}}) \subset V_{n,f}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We call these neighborhoods \emph{attracting}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Bounded distortion property for weakly contractive maps}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Following the approach in \cite{alves2000srb‎, ‎zaj2018physical}‎, ‎for maps $f\in \mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎we will provide a bounded distortion property on a region around its attracting fixed point $x_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We start with some preparatory results‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsubsection{Dominated splittings‎, ‎cone fields‎, ‎and the strong stable foliation}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given $f\in \mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎consider the $Df$-invariant splitting $E^{ss} \oplus E^{c}$‎, ‎corresponding to the eigenspaces of $Df(x_f)$ associated to the eigenvalues less than $1$ and equal to $1$‎, ‎respectively‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that $E^c$ is one-dimensional‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎As this splitting is dominated‎, ‎we can extend it to a continuous splitting (also denoted by $E^{ss}\oplus E^{c}$) defined on a small attracting neighborhood $V_f$ of $x_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that this new splitting is not necessarily invariant‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For notational convenience‎, ‎we will use the adapted metric (equivalent to the initial one) provided by \cite[Theorem 1]{gourmelon2007adapted}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This allows us to take the constant $C$ in equation \eqref{ce} equal to $1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Hence‎, ‎there exists $0&lt; \lambda&lt;1$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item $\|D f|_{E^{ss}_x}\| \leq \lambda$‎, ‎for all $x \in \overline{V_f}$;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item $\|D f|_{E^{ss}_x}\| \cdot \|D f^{-1}|_{E^{c}_{f(x)}}\| \leq \lambda&lt;1$ for all $x \in \overline{V_f}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Recalling that $x_f$ is parabolic‎, ‎by shrinking $V_f$ and slightly increasing $\lambda&lt;1$ if necessary‎, ‎we have the following strong form of domination‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{strongversionii}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\|D f|_{E^{ss}_x}\| \leq \lambda \quad \text{and} \quad \|D f|_{E^{ss}_x}\| \cdot \|D f^{-1}|_{E^{c}_{f(x)}}\|^{1+1} \leq \lambda&lt; 1‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for all $x \in \overline{V_f}$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For each $0&lt;\beta&lt;1$‎, ‎we define the \emph{center cone field} $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}:= (\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}(y))_{y \in \overline{V_f}}$ of width $\beta$ by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}(y):=\{v_1‎ +‎v_2 \in E^{ss}_y \oplus E^{c}_y‎ : ‎\|v_1\| \leq \beta \|v_2\|\}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The strong stable cone field $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{ss}:= (\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{ss}(y))_{y \in V_f}$ of width $\beta$ is analogously defined‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The center cone field is positively invariant‎, ‎that is $Df(y)( \mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}(y)) \subset \mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}(f(y))$‎, ‎provided that $V_f$ is small and $y‎, ‎f(y)\in \overline{V_f}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Indeed‎, ‎according to (ii) above‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎Df(x_f)(\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}(x_f)) \subset \mathcal{C}_{\lambda \beta} ^{c}(x_f) \subset \mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}(x_f)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By continuity‎, ‎this extends (for small $V_f$) to each $y \in \overline{V_f} \cap f^{-1}(\overline{V_f})$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By \cite{brin1974partially‎, ‎hirsch1977invariant}‎, ‎the domination property and the uniform contraction of $E^{ss}$ imply that there is a (uniquely defined) continuous family $\mathcal{F}^{ss}$ of injectively $C^2$ immersed submanifolds $\{\mathcal{F}^{ss}(x) \colon x \in V_f \}$ of dimension $(n-1)$ such that $x \in \mathcal{F}^{ss}(x)$ and $\mathcal{F}^{ss}(x)$ is tangent to $E^{ss}_x$ at $x$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This family is invariant‎, ‎in the sense that $f(\mathcal{F}^{ss}(x))\subset\mathcal{F}^{ss}(f(x))$ for every $x \in V_f \cap f^{-1}(V_f)$‎, ‎and the leaves $\mathcal{F}^{ss}(x)$ are uniformly contracted by $f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The lamination $\mathcal{F}^{ss}$ is called \emph{strong stable foliation} and the sets $\mathcal{F}^{ss}(x)$ are called \emph{strong stable leaves}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since the central bundle $E^{c}$ is one-dimensional‎, ‎there always exist curves tangent to $E^c$ passing through every point in $V_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎These curves are called \emph{central leaves} and denoted by $\mathcal{F}^{c}(x)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In general‎, ‎these central curves are neither invariant nor uniquely defined‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We now focus on these central curves and their iterations‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsubsection{Curves tangent to the central cone field and their iterations}\label{sss.curvestangento}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The next remark is a standard consequence of the persistence of domination and invariant bundles‎, ‎see for instance \cite[Section B1]{bonatti2004dynamics}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{remark}\label{pert}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎its attracting parabolic fixed point $x_f$‎, ‎and a small attracting neighbourhood $V_f$ of $x_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎After increasing $\lambda &lt;1$ and shrinking $V_f$‎, ‎we can assume that the dominated splitting $E^{ss} \oplus E^{c}$ is defined on $\overline{V_f}$ and satisfies \eqref{strongversionii} and there is small $\beta&gt;0$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{e3}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\|Df(y)v^{ss}\| \cdot \|Df^{-1}(f(y))v^{c}\| \leq \lambda \|v^{ss}\| \cdot \|v^{c}\|‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for every pair of vectors $v^{ss}\in \mathcal{C}^{ss}_\beta(y)$‎, ‎$v^c \in \mathcal{C}^c_\beta(y)$ and every point $y \in \overline{V_f} \cap f^{-1}(\overline{V_f})$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎we can assume that there exists a $C^2$-neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_f$ of $f$ consisting of maps $g$ such that $g(\overline V_f) \subset V_f$‎, ‎the domination conditions in \eqref{strongversionii} hold‎, ‎$\|D g|_{E^{ss}_x}\| \leq \lambda$‎, ‎the central cone field is $Dg$-invariant‎, ‎i.e.‎, ‎$Dg(y)\mathcal{C}^c_\beta(y)\subset \mathcal{C}^c_\beta(g(y))$‎, ‎and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{e4}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\|Dg(y)v^{ss}\| \cdot \|Dg^{-1}(g(y))v^{c}\| \leq \lambda \|v^{ss}\| \cdot \|v^{c}\|‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for every $v^{ss} \in \mathcal{C}_\beta ^{ss}(y)$‎, ‎$v^{c} \in \mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}(y)$‎, ‎and every $y \in \overline{V_f} \cap g^{-1}(\overline{V_f})$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎As a consequence‎, ‎the maximal invariant set of $g$ in $\overline{V_f}$ is partially hyperbolic with one dimensional central bundle and $(n-1)$-strong stable direction‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎we fix $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎neighbourhoods $V_f$ of $x_f$ and $\mathcal{N}_f$ of $f$‎, ‎and cone fields $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{ss}$ and $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}$ satisfying Remark~\ref{pert}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A closed $C^1$-curve $N\subset V_f$ is \emph{tangent to the central cone field $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}$} if for every $x\in N$ the tangent space $T_xN$ is contained in $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We call these curves \emph{central} ones‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A special case of central curves are the central leaves‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given a central curve $N$‎, ‎the invariance conditions in Remark~\ref{pert} imply that for every $g \in \mathcal{N}_f$ we have that $g(N) \subset V_f$ and $g(N)$ is tangent to $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Inductively‎, ‎we have that if $g_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎g_n \in \mathcal{N}_f$ then $g_n \circ \cdots \circ g_1 (N) \subset V_f$ and the curve $g_n \circ \cdots \circ g_1 (N) $ is a central curve‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Similarly‎, ‎we define $(n-1)$ disks tangent to the strong stable cone field and call them \emph{strong stable} disks‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Applying Remark~\ref{pert}‎, ‎we have that if $g \in \mathcal{N}_f$ and $D$ is a strong stable disk such that $D\subset V_f$ and $g^{-1}(D) \subset V_f$ then $g^{-1}(D)$ is a strong stable disk with $\diam (D) \le \lambda \diam (g^{-1} (D))$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Inductively‎, ‎if $g_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎g_n \in \mathcal{N}_f$ and $D\subset V_f$ is a strong stable disk such that $g_i^{-1} \circ \cdots \circ g_1^{-1} (D) \subset V_f$ for every $i=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎n$‎, ‎then $g_i^{-n} \circ \cdots \circ g_1^{-1} (D)$ is a strong stable disk such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{e.stablecompositions}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\diam (D) \le \lambda^n \diam (g_i^{-n} \circ \cdots \circ g_1^{-1} (D))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Finally‎, ‎given a set $A$ and a point $x\in A$‎, ‎we denote by $C(x,A)$ the connected component of $A$ containing $x$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We will consider the family of strong stable disks $\mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x):= C(x,\mathcal{F}^{ss} (x)\cap V_f)$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsubsection{Random iterations of central curves}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{sss.iterationsofcentralcurves}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this subsection‎, ‎we closely follow the constructions in \cite[Section 2.1]{alves2000srb} and omit some details‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given a central curve $N$ as above‎, ‎$x\in N$‎, ‎and $\epsilon&gt;0$‎, ‎let $U_x (\epsilon)$ be the open $\epsilon$-neighborhood of $x$ in $\mathbb{B}_n$ and $N_x(\epsilon)‎ :‎= U_x(\epsilon) \cap N$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎There is small $r&gt;0$ such that for each $x\in N$ there is a uniquely defined family of linear maps‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\mathcal{A}_x(y) \colon T_x N_x \to E_x^{ss}‎, ‎\quad y \in N_x(r)‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎such that the tangent spaces $T_y N_x$ are parallel to the graphs of $\mathcal{A}_x(y)$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For given constants $C&gt; 0$ and $0&lt; \zeta \leq 1$‎, ‎we say that the tangent bundle of $N$ is $(C‎, ‎\zeta)$-\emph{H\&quot;{o}lder} if there is $r&gt;0$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{L}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\|\mathcal{A}_x(y)\| \leq C \rho(x‎, ‎y)^{\zeta} \quad \textnormal{for every $x\in N$ and $y \in N_x (r)$}‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where $\rho(x‎, ‎y)$ is the length of the curve connecting $x$ to $y$ in $N_x(r)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For $0&lt; \zeta \leq 1$ we let‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{e.curvaturek}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\kappa_\zeta(N):=\inf \{C &gt; 0‎: ‎\text{the tangent bundle of $N$ is $(C‎, ‎\zeta)$-H\&quot;{o}lder}\}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In \cite{alves2000srb} a H\&quot;older exponent $\zeta$ is selected satisfying‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\|D f|_{E^{ss}_x}\| \cdot (\|D f^{-1}|_{E^{c}_{f(x)}}\|)^{1+\zeta} \leq \lambda&lt;1‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By equation \eqref{strongversionii}‎, ‎we can take $\zeta=1$ and write $\kappa_1(N)= \kappa(N)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Next proposition is just a reformulation of \cite[Corollary 2.4]{alves2000srb} in our context of random compositions‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For that let us introduce some notation‎: ‎given a sequence $\mathbf{t}=(\cdots‎, ‎t_{-j}‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0)$ and maps $f_{t_{-i}}\colon \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎for each $j\ge 0$ we write‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{e.compositionsoffs}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎f^{j+1}_\mathbf{t}‎ :‎= f_{t_0}\circ f_{t_{-1}}\circ \cdots \circ f_{t_{-j}}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proposition}[Corollary 2.4 in \cite{alves2000srb}]\label{proj}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎There are small neighborhoods $V_f$ of $x_f$ and $\mathcal{N}_f$ of $f$‎, ‎a constant $\beta &gt;0$ satisfying Remark~\ref{pert}‎, ‎and a constant $C_1 &gt; 0$ such that for every $C^1$-curve $N \subset V_f$ tangent to the centre cone field $\mathcal{C}_\beta ^{c}$‎, ‎every continuous path $t \in I \mapsto f_t \in \mathcal{N}_f$‎, ‎with $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ an interval‎, ‎and every sequence $\mathbf{t}=(\cdots‎, ‎t_{-j}‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0)$ with values in $I$ the following holds‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item [$a$)] there exists $n_0 \geq 0$ such that $\kappa(f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n}(N)) \leq C_1$ for every $n \geq n_0$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item [$b$)] if $\kappa(N)\leq C_1$ then the same is true for every $\kappa(f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n}(N))$‎, ‎$n \geq 0$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item [$c$)] in particular‎, ‎if $N$ is as in b)‎, ‎then the Jacobian functions‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎J^n_{\mathbf{t},N} \colon f_{\mathbf{t}}^n(N) \ni y \mapsto \log |\det(D f |_{T_y f_{\mathbf{t}}^n(N)})|‎, ‎\quad n \geq 0‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎are $(L,1)$-H\&quot;{o}lder continuous‎, ‎for some $L &gt; 0$ depending only on $C_1$ and $f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proposition}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A special case of Proposition~\ref{proj} is the non-random case where $f_{t_{-i}}=g\in \mathcal{N}_f$ for every $i$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsubsection{Cusp-like regions}\label{cr}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎a center leaf $\mathcal{F}^{c}(x_f)$ passing throughout $x_f$‎, ‎and a closed fundamental domain $D^c$ of $\mathcal{F}^{c}(x_f)$ with boundary points $a\in V_f$ and $f(a)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For each $x\in D^c$ consider a small closed strong stable disk $D^{ss}(x)\subset \mathcal{F}^{ss}(x)$ containing the point $x$ in its interior and contained in $V_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We choose these disks depending continuously on $x$ and such that $D^{ss}(f(a)):=f(D^{ss}(a))$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Denote by $C^2(D^c‎, ‎V_f)$ the space of $C^2$ maps from $D^c$ to $V_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $H\colon D^{ss}(a) \to C^2(D^c‎, ‎V_f)$ be a continuous map which associates to each $x \in D^{ss}(a)$ a $C^2$ arc $I_x:=H(x)(D^c)$ connecting the points $x$ and $f(x)$ such that $H(a)$ is the identity and hence $I_a=D^c$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎by shrinking the disks $D^{ss}(x)$‎, ‎if necessary‎, ‎we can assume that the curve $I_x$ is a central curve for every $x \in D^{ss}(a)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider now the closed subset‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{w1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎W_1:= \bigcup_{x \in D^{ss}(a)}I_x \subset V_f‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎foliated by central curves and whose boundary contains the disks $D^{ss}(a)$ and $D^{ss}(f(a))$‎, ‎see Figure~\ref{fig:cuspregion}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By construction‎, ‎the subsets $W_1$ and $f(W_1)$ are $C^2$ submanifolds contained in $V_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Arguing recursively‎, ‎we define the \emph{cusp-like region} $W$ as follows‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{r02}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎W:=\bigcup_{k=1}^\infty W_k‎, ‎\quad \textnormal{where } W_k:=f(W_{k-1}),\quad k\ge 2‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{figure}[!ht]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{center}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\includegraphics[scale=1.3]{blend-fig.pdf}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\caption{\small{The closed rectangle $W_1$.}}\label{fig:cuspregion}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{center}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{figure}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{remark}\label{r2}{\em{‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that $\diam(W_k)$ tends to zero as $k \to \infty$ and that the parabolic point $x_f$ satisfies $x_f \in \overline{W} \setminus W$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Finally‎, ‎the subsets $W_k$ and $W$ are $C^2$ submanifolds.}}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Taking $f_{t_{-i}}=f$ for all $i$ in Proposition \ref{proj}‎, ‎we have that the tangent bundle of the iterates $f^n(I_x)$ are H\&quot;{o}lder continuous with uniform H\&quot;older constants depending only on $f$ and $\kappa_1(D^c)$‎, ‎see \eqref{e.curvaturek}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎by continuity of $H$ and compactness of $I_x$ and $D^{ss}(a)$‎, ‎we have that $\kappa_1(I_x)$ is uniformly bounded independently of $x$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In particular‎, ‎by item (c) of Proposition \ref{proj}‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{ten}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎J^k_{I_x} \colon f^k(I_x) \ni y \mapsto \log |\det(Df|_{T_yf^k(I_x)})|‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is $(L‎, ‎1)$-H\&quot;{o}lder continuous‎, ‎for some constant $L &gt; 0$ that does not depend on $k$ and $x$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The observations above are summarized in the next proposition‎, ‎which is a counterpart of \cite[Thm.~2.7]{zaj2018physical} in the $n$-dimensional case‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proposition}\label{thmcusp}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$ and consider sufficiently small cusp-like region $W$ defined as in \eqref{r02}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then the function‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎J‎ : ‎W \ni y \mapsto \log |\det(Df|_{T_y(W)})|‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is $(L,1)$-H\&quot;{o}lder continuous‎, ‎for some constant $L &gt; 0$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proposition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsubsection{Random limits of a ball }\label{sss.randomlimits}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this section‎, ‎we prove the following result about forward random iterations of the ball $\mathbb{B}_n$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proposition}\label{open}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎There is a $C^2$ neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_f$ of $f$ such that for every continuous path $t \in I \mapsto f_t \in \mathcal{N}_f$ and every sequence $\mathbf{t}=(\cdots‎, ‎t_{-n}‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0)$ with values in $I$‎, ‎the intersection‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}= \bigcap_{k\ge 0} \mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{t}} (k)‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\qquad‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{t}} (k)‎ :‎= f^k_{\mathbf{t}} (\mathbb{B}_{n})‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is either a single point or a one dimensional $C^1$ closed submanifold‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proposition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The definition of $f^k_{\mathbf{t}}$ in \eqref{e.compositionsoffs} implies that $(\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{t}} (k))_k$ is a nested sequence of compact and connected sets‎, ‎therefore its intersection $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is compact and connected‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take neighborhoods $V_f$ of $x_f$ and $\mathcal{N}_f$ of $f$ as in Remark \ref{pert}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fix a center leaf $\mathcal{F}^{c}(x_f)$ passing throughout $x_f$ and let $\mathcal{F}^{c}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)= C(x_f‎, ‎\mathcal{F}^{c}(x_f)\cap V_f)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We consider the neighborhood of $x_f$ given by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{v0}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎V=\bigcup_{x \in \mathcal{F}^{c}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)} \mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x) \subset V_f‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where $\mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x):=\mathcal{F}^{ss}(x)\cap V_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By shrinking $V_f$ and $\mathcal{N}_f$‎, ‎we can assume that $g(\overline{V}) \subset \mathrm{int} (V)$ for all $g\in \mathcal{N}_f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that $V$ is foliated by stable curves‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎After shrinking the neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_f$ it is enough to prove the proposition for $V$‎: ‎just note that there is $n_0$ with $f^{n_0} (\mathbb{B}_n) \subset \mathrm{int}(V)$‎, ‎thus taking $\mathcal{N}_f$ small enough we also have that $f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n_0} (\mathbb{B}_n) \subset \mathrm{int}(V)$ for every sequence $\mathbf{t}$ as in the proposition‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{l.atmostapoint}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For every sequence $\mathbf{t}$ as in the proposition and every point $x \in \mathcal{F}^{c}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$ the intersection $\mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x) \cap \Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is either a point or empty‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎As the set $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is connected‎, ‎its projection in $\mathcal{F}^{c}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$ along the strong stable leaves is a closed curve (maybe degenerate to a point)‎, ‎hence $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is a continuous graph over that curve‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\ref{l.atmostapoint}]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given a point $x\in \Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ consider the sequences of points $x_k = f_{\mathbf{t}}^{-k}(x) \in V$ and of discs‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎C(x_k‎, ‎f_{\textbf{t}}^{-k}(\mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x))\cap V) = \Delta^{ss}_{\mathbf{t}} (x_k)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By the comments in Section~\ref{sss.curvestangento}‎, ‎the set $\Delta^{ss}_{\mathbf{t}} (x_k)$ is a stable disc and there is a constant $C&gt;0$ independent of $x$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\diam (\Delta_k(x)) \le C \lambda^k‎, ‎\quad \mbox{where } \quad \Delta_k(x)= f_{\textbf{t}}^{k} (\Delta^{ss}_{\mathbf{t}} (x_k))\subset \mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This immediately implies that the intersection $\mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}} (x)\cap \Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ consists of at most one point‎, ‎proving the lemma‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎If $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is a single point‎, ‎we are done‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Otherwise‎, ‎since $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is a continuous graph over a curve in $\mathcal{F}^{c}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$‎, ‎thus $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is a closed curve‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎So‎, ‎it remains to see that this curve is $C^1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎To see why this is so‎, ‎denote by $C^2(\mathcal{F}^c_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)‎, ‎V)$ the space of $C^2$ maps from $\mathcal{F}^c_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$ to $V$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $H \colon \mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}} (x_f)\to C^2(\mathcal{F}^c_{\mathrm{loc}} (x_f)‎, ‎V)$ be a continuous map which associates to each $x \in \mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$ a $C^2$ arc $N_x‎ :‎= H(x)(\mathcal{F}^c_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f))$ such that $H(x_f)$ is the identity and hence $N_{x_f} = \mathcal{F}^c_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎by shrinking the neighborhood $V$ if necessary‎, ‎we can assume that the curve $N_x$ is tangent to the center cone field $\mathcal{C}^c_{\beta}$ for every $x \in \mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider now the closed subset‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{a11}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\widetilde{V}:=\bigcup_{x \in \mathcal{F}^{ss}_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)}N_x \subset V‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎So‎, ‎we may assume that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{a22}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}= \bigcap_{k\ge 0}f^k_{\mathbf{t}}(\widetilde{V})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Using Proposition \ref{proj}‎, ‎the tangent bundle of the random iterates $f_{\textbf{t}}^k(N_x)$ are H\&quot;{o}lder continuous with uniform H\&quot;older constants depending only on $\kappa(\mathcal{F}^c_{loc}(x_f))$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎by continuity of $H$ and compactness of $N_x$ and $\mathcal{F}^c_{\mathrm{loc}}(x_f)$‎, ‎$\kappa(N_x)$ has a uniform bound independent of $x$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Also‎, ‎by item (c) of Proposition \ref{proj}‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{a33}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎J^k_{\textbf{t},N_x} \colon f_{\textbf{t}}^k(N_x) \ni y \mapsto \log |\det(Df|_{T_yf_{\textbf{t}}^k(N_x)})|‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is $(L‎, ‎1)$-H\&quot;{o}lder continuous‎, ‎for some $L &gt; 0$ that does not depend on $k$ and $x$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By (\ref{a22}) and (\ref{a33})‎, ‎since $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is a closed curve‎, ‎and using the graph transform approach‎, ‎$\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is a $C^1$ curve‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}\label{nhyper}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$ and $\mathcal{N}_f$ be the neighborhood of $f$ that satisfies the conclusion of Proposition \ref{open}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then‎, ‎we say the $\mathcal{N}_f$ is a normally hyperbolic adapted $C^2$ neighborhood of $f$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{remark}\label{p1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$ and take the neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_f$ of $f$ as in Proposition \ref{open}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then for every $g \in \mathcal{N}_f$ the following trichotomy holds‎: ‎$g$ is either a weakly contractive map having a generic parabolic attracting fixed point $x_g$ or a uniformly contracting map or has a normally contracting one dimensional submanifold $\Gamma_g\subset \mathbb{B}_n$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{theorem}\label{open2}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $f\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$ and $\mathcal{N}_f$ be a normally hyperbolic adapted $C^2$ neighborhood of $f$ as in Proposition \ref{open}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then for every sequence $\mathbf{t}=(\cdots‎, ‎t_{-n}‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0)$ with values in $I$‎, ‎such that for each $n$‎, ‎$f_{t_{-n}} \in \mathcal{N}_f$ or $f_{t_{-n}}$ is a $C^2$ injective map with $f_{t_{-n}}(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset \text{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎the intersection‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}= \bigcap_{k\ge 0} \mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{t}} (k)‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\qquad‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{t}} (k)‎ :‎= f^k_{\mathbf{t}} (\mathbb{B}_{n})‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is either a single point or a one dimensional $C^1$ closed submanifold‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{theorem}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take the neighborhood $V$ of $x_f$ given by \eqref{v0} and the normally hyperbolic adapted $C^2$ neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_f$ of $f$‎, ‎and let‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\mathcal{A}=\{n \in \mathbb{N}‎: ‎f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n} (\mathbb{B}_n) \subset \mathrm{int}(V)\}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎There are two cases‎:</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎1) $\mathcal{A}$ is finite‎. ‎Let $n_0$ be the maximum of $\mathcal{A}$‎. ‎Then‎, ‎for each $n &gt; n_0$‎, ‎$f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n} (\mathbb{B}_n)$ intersects the complement $\mathbb{B}_n \setminus V$‎. ‎Thus‎, ‎by construction‎, ‎$f_{\textbf{t}_{-n-1}}$ is uniformly contracting on $f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n} (\mathbb{B}_n)$ and hence‎, ‎$\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is a single point‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎2) $\mathcal{A}$ is infinite‎. ‎Since $\mathcal{A}$ is countable‎, ‎we can write the elements of $\mathcal{A}$ as a sequence $\{n_k‎: ‎k \in \mathbb{N}\}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎for each $k$‎, ‎$f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n_k} (\mathbb{B}_n)\subset \mathrm{int}(V)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We take the sequence $\mathbf{s}=(\cdots‎, ‎s_{-k}‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎s_{-1}‎, ‎s_0)$ with values in $I$ and define $g_{s_{-k}}$ by $g_{s_0}=f_{\mathbf{t}}^{n_1}$‎, ‎and $g_{s_{-k}}=f_{t_{-n_k‎ -‎1}}\circ \dots \circ f_{t_{-n_{k‎ +1}}}$, ‎for each $k \geq 1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then‎, ‎by definition‎, ‎we can apply the argument used in Proposition \ref{open} to the maps $g_{s_{-k}}$‎, ‎$k\geq 0$‎, ‎we find that the set $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$ is equal to $\bigcap_{k\ge 0}\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{s}} (k)$‎, ‎where $\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{s}} (k) = g^k_{\mathbf{s}} (\mathbb{B}_{n})$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎$\bigcap_{k\ge 0}\mathbb{B}_{\mathbf{s}} (k)$ is either a single point or a one dimensional $C^1$ closed submanifold and the same holds for $\Gamma_{\mathbf{t}}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This finishes the proof‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\section{Weakly blending regions}\label{wbr}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We now introduce two local tools used to construct porcupine-like attractors‎: ‎the covering property and the weak blending region‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}[Covering property \cite{nassiri2012robust}]\label{covering}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A finite collection $\mathcal{F}=\{f_1‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎f_k\}$ of continuous maps $f_i \colon \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{B}_n$ satisfies the \emph{covering property} with respect to an open ball $B \subset \mathrm{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$ if‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\mathrm{clos}(B)\subset f_1(B)\cup \ldots \cup f_k(B)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this case‎, ‎we say that the set $B$ is a \emph{blending region of $\mathcal{F}$}‎. ‎This blending region is \emph{uniformly contracting} if the restriction of $f_i$ to $\mathrm{clos}(B)$ is uniformly contracting for every $i = 1‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎k$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎we denote by $\mathrm{Inj}^2 (\mathbb{B}_n)$ the set of $C^2$ injective endomorphisms $f$ of $\mathbb{B}_n$ satisfying $f(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset \text{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}[Weakly attracting pair]\label{weakpair}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider an open set $B \subset \mathrm{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$ and a finite family of maps $\mathcal{F}=\{f_1‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎f_k\}$‎, ‎$k\ge 3$‎, ‎with $f_i \in \mathrm{Inj}^2 (\mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We say that $(B,\mathcal{F})$ is a \emph{weakly attracting pair} if the following conditions hold‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item[(H1)] $f_1 \in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$ with parabolic fixed point $x_{f_1}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item[(H2)] For $i\ge 2$‎, ‎the map $f_{i}$ is uniformly contracting‎. ‎In particular‎, ‎$f_i$ has a unique fixed point denoted by $x_{f_i}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item[(H3)]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The following contraction on average property holds‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{3333}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\sum_{i=1}^{k} \log \|D f_{i}(x)\| &lt;0‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for every $x\in \mathbb{B}_n$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item [(H4)] The open set $B$ is a (uniformly contracting) blending region for $\{f_2‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎f_k\}$ satisfying‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\mathrm{clos}(B) \cap \{x_{f_1}‎, ‎x_{f_2}‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎x_{f_k}\} =\emptyset‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎If $(B,\mathcal{F})$ is a weakly attracting pair then we say that $B$ is a \emph{weakly blending region} of $\mathcal{F}$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A pair $(B,\mathcal{G})$ is \emph{weakly repelling} if $(B,\mathcal{G}^{-1})$ is weakly attracting‎, ‎where $\mathcal{G}^{-1}=\{g^{-1} \colon g \in \mathcal{G}\}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{theorem}\label{problend}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For every $n\ge 1$‎, ‎there are an open ball $B$ with $\mathrm{clos}(B) \subset \mathrm{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$ and a finite family of maps $\mathcal{F}$ such that $(B‎, ‎\mathcal{F})$ is a weakly attracting pair‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{theorem}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\mathbb{B}_n$ be the closed unit ball containing the origin‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take a point $x_0 \in \mathbb{B}_n$ on the $x_n$-axis and a smooth function $\alpha:\mathbb{B}_n \to [1/2,1]$ such that $\alpha(x_0)=1$‎, ‎$\alpha(x)\neq1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{B}_n \setminus \{x_0\}$‎, ‎and $\alpha (x) =1/2$ if $\|x\| \ge 1/2$‎. ‎Let $A(x)$‎, ‎$x \in \mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎be a diagonal matrix with $A(x)=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎\lambda_{n-1}‎, ‎\alpha(x))$‎, ‎where $0&lt; \lambda_i &lt;1/3$ and $\lambda_i \neq \lambda_j$‎, ‎for every $i,j \in \{1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎n-1\}$ with $i\neq j$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take the diffeomorphism $\varphi \colon \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\varphi(x_0)=x_0‎, ‎\quad \mbox{and} \quad D\varphi(x)=A(x)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then $\varphi$ has a generic parabolic attracting fixed point at $x_0$ whose one dimensional central bundle $E^{c}$ is the $x_n$-axis‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎if $x_0$ is close enough to the origin the restriction map $f_1=\varphi_{| \mathbb{B}_n}$ belongs to $\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Associated to $f_1$ we consider a region $W_1$ as in \eqref{w1} and its associated cusp-like region‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$W=\bigcup_{k=1}^\infty W_k$‎, ‎where $W_k=f_1(W_{k-1})$ for $k\geq 2$‎, ‎as in \eqref{r02}‎. ‎Taking $x_0$ close enough to the origin and choosing $W$ on the appropriate side of $x_0$ (in the central leaf)‎, ‎we can assume that $\text{int}(W)$ contains the origin‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that $x_0 \not\in \text{int}(W)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎the map $f_1$ and the region $W$ satisfy the conclusion of Proposition \ref{thmcusp}‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{figure}[h]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{center}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\vspace{-.5cm}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\includegraphics[scale=1.3]{new-fig.pdf}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\caption{\small{The covering property‎: ‎The cusp-like region $W$ and the parabolic fixed point $x_0$ of $f$‎. ‎The blending region $B$ and their images $f_i(B)$.}}\label{fig:2}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\vspace{-.5cm}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{center}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{figure}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider now the open ball $B=B(0,\delta)$‎, ‎small $\delta &gt;0$ with $B (0,5\delta)\subset \text{int}(W)$‎. ‎Hence $x_0\not\in B$‎. ‎See Figure \ref{fig:2}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider now a contraction $g \in \mathrm{Inj}^2 (\mathbb{B}_n)$ whose restriction to the ball $B(0,4\,\delta)$ is given by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎g(x_1‎, ‎\dots,x_n)= \frac{9}{10} (x_1,\dots‎, ‎x_n)‎ + ‎(0,\dots,0‎, ‎\delta/5)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then $p_\delta=(0‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎0,2\,\delta)\not\in B$ is the fixed point of $g$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎the origin belongs to $g(B) \cap B$ (note that $g(0,\dots,0‎, ‎-10\,\delta/45)=(0,\dots,0)$),‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎and a neighbourhood of the segment $\{0^{n-1}\} \times [0‎, ‎\delta]$ is contained in $g(B) \cap B$‎. ‎This implies that there is a family of linear isometries $A_2‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎A_k$ which fixes $0$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{e.Biscontained}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎B\subset \bigcup_{i=2}^k A_i(g(B))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By \cite{conradisometries}‎, ‎if $A$ is an isometry of $\mathbb{R}^n$ that fixes 0 then there is an orthogonal decomposition $ \mathbb{R}^n = W_1 \bigoplus W_2 \bigoplus \dots \bigoplus W_m$ such that $dim(W_i) = 1$ or 2 for all $i$‎, ‎and the restriction of $A$ to $W_i$ is a rotation unless $i = m$‎, ‎$dim(W_m) = 1$‎, ‎and $\text{det} A =‎ -1$, ‎in which case the restriction of $A$ to $W_m$ is a reflection‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For each $i$ let‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎f_i = A_i \circ g \circ A_i^{-1}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The above fact implies that the map $f_i\in \mathrm{Inj}^2 (\mathbb{B}_n)$ is a contraction whose fixed point $A_i(p_\delta)$ has modulus $2\,\delta$ and hence does not belong to $B$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this way we obtain (H1)--(H3)‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎To complete (H4)‎, ‎note that $B=A_i (B)$‎, ‎hence‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎f_i(B) = A_i \circ g \circ A_i^{-1}(B) = A_i \circ g \circ A_i^{-1}(A_i(B)) = A_i (g (B))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎by \eqref{e.Biscontained}‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎B \subset \bigcup_{i=2}^k f_i(B)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This implies that $B$ is a blending region disjoint from the fixed points of $f_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎f_k$‎, ‎proving the theorem‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎According to the above arguments‎, ‎we will have the following corollary‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{corollary} \label{c.weakblending}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider a weakly attracting pair $(B,\mathcal{F})$‎, ‎where $\mathcal{F}=\{f_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎f_k\}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then there exist $C^2$ neighbourhoods $\mathcal{N}_i$ of $f_i$‎, ‎$i=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎k$‎, ‎such that for every family $\mathcal{G}=\{g_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎g_k\}$‎, ‎with $g_i \in \mathcal{N}_i$‎, ‎the pair $(B‎, ‎\mathcal{G})$ satisfies conditions (H2)-(H4) in Definition~\ref{weakpair}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎by Proposition \ref{open}‎, ‎$g_1$ is either a weakly contractive map or a uniformly contracting map or it is normally contracting along some one dimensional submanifold of $\mathbb{B}_n$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{corollary}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\section{Skew products and geometrical structures of their attracting invariant graphs}\label{sec3}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Invariant graphs and maximal attractors}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎As mentioned earlier‎, ‎we now focus on skew products having non-uniform contraction rates along the fibers‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎we consider compact metric spaces $B$ and $Y$ and continuous maps $h \colon B \to B$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fixed $h$‎, ‎as before‎, ‎we denote by $\mathcal{C}_{h,\Lip}(B\times Y)$ the set of skew products‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\label{e.asin}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎F=F_{(h,f_b)} \colon B\times Y \to B \times Y‎, ‎\qquad‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎F(b,y) =(h(b)‎, ‎f_b(y))‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎whose fiber maps $f_b$‎, ‎$b \in B$‎, ‎are Lipschitz and continuously depend on $b$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎we let‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\Delta (Y)‎ : ‎= \{(y,y') \in Y \times Y \colon y\ne y' \}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}[Contracting on average‎, ‎\cite{stark1999regularity}]\label{average}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider a skew product $F_{(h‎, ‎f_b)}$ as above and a $h$-invariant measure $m$‎. ‎We say that $F$ is \emph{$m$-contracting on average} if the limit‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{777}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\lambda(b)‎ : ‎= \lim_{n \to \infty}\frac{1}{n}\sup_{(y,y^{\prime})\in \Delta (Y)}\log \frac{d(f_b^n(y)‎, ‎f_b^n(y^{\prime}))}{d(y,y^{\prime})}‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎exists for $m$-almost every $b$‎, ‎the map $b \mapsto \lambda(b)$ is measurable‎, ‎and there is $\lambda&lt;0$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{landa}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\lambda(b)\leq \lambda‎, ‎\quad \text{for $m$-almost every $b$.}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In our context‎, ‎the importance of the contracting on average property is illustrated by the following theorem‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{theorem}{\em{(\cite[Theorem 1.4]{stark1999regularity})}}\label{thmg}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider compact metric spaces $B$ and $Y$‎, ‎a homeomorphism $h\colon B \to B$‎, ‎a $h$-invariant ergodic measure $m$‎, ‎and an $m$-contracting on average skew product $F\in \mathcal{C}_{h‎, ‎\Lip}(B\times Y)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then there exist a $h$-invariant set $\Lambda \subset B$ with $m(\Lambda)= 1$ and a function $\gamma \colon \Lambda \to Y$ whose graph is invariant and attracting under $F$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{theorem}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Denote by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{ccc}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\mathcal{C}^2_h(B \times Y)\subset \mathcal{C}_{h‎, ‎\Lip} (B\times Y)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎the set of skew products whose fiber maps $f_b$‎, ‎$b \in B$‎, ‎are $C^2$ and continuously depend on $b$ with respect to $C^2$ topology‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{cor0}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $F=F_{(h,f_b)} \in \mathcal{C}^2_h(B \times Y)$ be a $m$-contracting on average skew product with respect to a $h$-invariant ergodic measure $m$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then there exists an open neighborhood $\mathcal{V}(F) \subset \mathcal{C}^2_h(B \times Y)$ of $F$ consisting of $m$-contracting on average skew products‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since the map $(b,y)\mapsto f_b(y)$ is continuous and $C^2$ in $y$‎, ‎there exists $\varsigma \in L^1(B,m)$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎d(f_b(y),f_b(y^{\prime}))&lt;\varsigma(b)\,d(y,y^{\prime})‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for $m$-almost every $b$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then‎, ‎by \cite[Lem.~5.1]{stark1999regularity}‎, ‎for every $n\geq 0$ the number‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{n-lya}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\varsigma_{{F},n}(b)\eqdef \varsigma_n(b) \eqdef \sup_{(y,y^{\prime})\in \Delta (Y)}\frac{d(f_b^n(y)‎ , ‎f_b^n(y^{\prime}))}{d(y,y^{\prime})}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎exists for almost every $b$ and the map $\varsigma_n$ is measurable‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Also‎, ‎by \cite[Lem.~5.3]{stark1999regularity}‎, ‎the sequence $\varsigma_n$ is submultiplicative and hence $\log \varsigma_n$ is subadditive‎, ‎that is‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\log \varsigma_{n+m}(b)\leq \log \varsigma_n(b)‎+ ‎\log \varsigma_m(h^n(b))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We can thus apply Kingman's subadditive ergodic theorem \cite[Thm.~5.4]{krengel2011ergodic} to $\log \varsigma_n$‎, ‎obtaining that the map‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{lya}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\lambda(b)=\lim_{n \to‎ +‎\infty}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_n(b)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎exists for $m$-almost every $b$ and is $h$ invariant‎. ‎Moreover‎, ‎by subadditivity‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{44}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\lim_{n \to \infty}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_n(b)= \inf_{n}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_n(b)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎and‎, ‎by ergodicity of $m$‎, ‎it is constant $m$-a.e.‎, ‎say equal to $\lambda_{F}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since ${F}$ is contracting on average we have that $\lambda_{{F}} &lt;0$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fix small $\delta&gt;0$ and take any ${G}={G}_{(h,g_b)} \in \mathcal{C}^2_{h}(B\times Y)$ that is $\delta$-close to ${F}$ with respect to the metric in (\ref{e113})‎, ‎that is‎, ‎$\sup_{b} \text{dist}_{C^2}(f_b^{\pm}‎, ‎g_b^{\pm})&lt; \delta$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We claim that‎, ‎for small $\delta$‎, ‎$G$ is contracting on average with respect to $m$‎. ‎Indeed‎, ‎as above‎, ‎the sequence‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{per}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\varsigma_{{G},n} (b)\eqdef \sup_{(y,y^{\prime}\in \Delta (Y))}\frac{d(g_b^n(y)‎ , ‎g_b^n(y^{\prime}))}{d(y,y^{\prime})}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is subadditive and hence both statements (\ref{lya}) and (\ref{44}) hold for $\log \varsigma_{{G},n}$ (where in (\ref{lya}) we consider $\lambda_G(b)$)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In particular‎, ‎by the ergodicity of $m$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\lim_{n \to‎ +‎\infty}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_{{G},n}(b)= \inf_{n}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_{{G},n}(b)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is constant $m$-a.e.‎, ‎say equal to $\lambda_{{G}}$‎. ‎To prove the lemma‎, ‎we need to see that $\lambda_{{G}}&lt;0$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fix small $\varepsilon&gt;0$ with $\lambda_{{F}}+2\varepsilon &lt;0$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By \eqref{44}‎, ‎$\lambda_{{F}}=\inf_{n}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_n(b)$‎, ‎for $m$-a.e‎. ‎$b$‎. ‎Thus‎, ‎for a typical point $b$ for $\lambda_{{F}}$ and $\lambda_{{G}}$‎, ‎there exists $n_0=n_0(b) \in \mathbb{N}$ such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\frac{1}{n_0}\log \varsigma_{{F}‎, ‎n_0}(b)&lt; \lambda_{{F}}+\varepsilon‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Taking $\delta$ small enough‎, ‎we get‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\frac{1}{n_0}\log \varsigma_{{G},n_0}(b)&lt; \lambda_{{F}}+2 \varepsilon &lt;0‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consequently‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\lambda_{{G}}=\inf_{n}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_{G,n}&lt; \frac{1}{n_0}\log \varsigma_{G,n_0}(b)&lt; \lambda_{{F}}‎ + ‎2\varepsilon &lt;0‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎proving that ${G}$ is contracting on average with respect to $m$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this section‎, ‎we introduce a class of skew products driven by an expanding circle map and their solenoidal extensions‎. ‎We show that maximal attractors of the extension skew products are either an attracting continuous invariant graph or a thick $n$-porcupine graph‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Skew products driven by expanding circle maps and their extensions}\label{section}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given $\ell\in \mathbb{N}$‎, ‎$\ell\ge 2$‎, ‎we consider the expanding map of the circle‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\varphi_\ell \colon \mathbb{T}^1 \to \mathbb{T}^1,\quad \varphi_\ell (t) = \ell t \ (\mathrm{mod\‎, ‎1})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take skew product maps defined on $\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n$ driven by $\varphi_\ell$ of the form‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{skewT1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎F_{(\ell‎, ‎f_t)} \colon \mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n‎, ‎\quad F (t,x)=(\varphi_\ell(t)‎, ‎f_t(x))‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where the fiber maps $f_t \colon \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎$t \in \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎are $C^2$ diffeomorphisms from the compact ball $\mathbb{B}_n$ onto their images such that $f_t(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset \text{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since the maps $\varphi_\ell$ are not invertible‎, ‎we consider their extensions given by the inverse limit construction in \cite{williams1974expanding}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For that consider the set‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\mathcal{S}_\ell‎ :‎=\{(\ldots,t_{-1},t_{0})\in (\mathbb{T}^1)^{\mathbb{Z}^-}‎: ‎t_{-j}=\varphi_\ell (t_{-j-1})\}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎endowed with the product topology‎. ‎The extension $\xi_\ell$ of $\varphi_\ell$ is defined by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}\label{3.9}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\xi_\ell \colon \mathcal{S}_\ell \to \mathcal{S}_\ell‎, ‎\quad \xi(\textbf{t})=(\ldots,t_{-1},t_{0},\varphi_\ell(t_0))‎, ‎\ \textnormal{for} \ \textbf{t}=(\ldots,t_{-1},t_{0})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The extension map $\xi_\ell$ is invertible and its inverse is given by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\xi_\ell^{-1}(\textbf{t})=(\ldots,t_{-2},t_{-1})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Finally‎, ‎consider the projection‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{e99}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\pi_\ell \colon \mathcal{S}_\ell \rightarrow \mathbb{T}^1‎, ‎\quad \pi(\textbf{t})=t_0‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that $\pi_\ell \circ \xi_\ell=\varphi_\ell \circ \pi_\ell$‎, ‎which means that $\pi_\ell$ is a semiconjugacy between $\varphi_\ell$ and $\xi_\ell$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The skew product map $F_{(\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$ in \eqref{skewT1} induces the skew product map $\mathbb{F}_{F}=\mathbb{F}_{(\xi_\ell‎, ‎f_{\textbf{t}})}$ on $\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎called the \emph{extension of $F$}‎, ‎defined by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{skew4}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\mathbb{F}_{F}(\textbf{t},x)=(\xi_\ell(\textbf{t}),f(\textbf{t},x))=(\xi_\ell(\textbf{t}),f_{\textbf{t}}(x))‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where $f_{\textbf{t}}(x):=f_{t_0}(x)$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{remark}\label{rem90}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The fiber maps of the skew product maps $F_{(\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$ and $\mathbb{F}_{F}$ are the same‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{remark}\label{circ-measure}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎On the circle $\mathbb{T}^1$ and the inverse limit spaces $\mathcal{S}_\ell$‎, ‎we use Borel $\sigma$-algebras‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that the expanding map $\varphi_\ell$ possesses an absolutely continuous invariant ergodic measure $\nu_\ell^+$ whose density is bounded away from zero and is equivalent to Lebesgue \cite{krzyzewski2004invariant‎, ‎mane2012ergodic}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The extension map $\xi_\ell$ has the invariant measure $\nu_\ell$ defined on $\mathcal{S}_\ell$ inherited from $\nu_\ell^+$ defined by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\nu_\ell (\{t_{-n}\in I_n‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎t_0 \in I_0\})‎ : ‎=\nu_\ell^+(\varphi_\ell^{-n}(I_0) \cap \varphi_\ell^{-n+1}(I_1)\cap \cdots \cap I_n)‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where $I_i$‎, ‎$i=0‎, ‎\cdots‎, ‎n,$ are intervals of $\mathbb{T}^1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{notations}\label{n.espacesofmaps}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The following notations are introduced‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item The space of all skew products $F_{(\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$ as in \eqref{skewT1} is denoted by $\mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎We denote by $\mathcal{C} (\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$ the space of skew product maps $\mathbb{F}= \mathbb{F}_{F}$ which are an extension of some $F\in \mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎We equip these spaces with the metric given by \eqref{e113}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item Given $F_{(\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$‎, ‎the set of all $F$-invariant probability measures on $\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n$ with marginal $\nu_\ell^+$ is denoted by $\mathcal{M}_{\nu_\ell^+}(F)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item Similarly‎, ‎for $\mathbb{F}= \mathbb{F}_{F}$‎, ‎we denote by $\mathcal{M}_{\nu_\ell}(\mathbb{F})$ the set of all $\mathbb{F}$-invariant probability measures on $\mathcal{S}_\ell\times \mathbb{B}_n$ with marginal $\nu_\ell$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{notations}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We equip the space $\mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n)$ with the following metric‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{e113}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\textnormal{dist}_{ \mathcal{C}_{\ell}}(F,G):=\sup_{t \in \mathbb{T}^1}(\textnormal{dist}_{C^2}(f_t^{\pm 1}‎ , ‎g_t^{\pm 1}))‎, ‎\ \textnormal{for each } F,G \in \mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1\times \mathbb{B}_n)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The following lemma \cite{crauel1990extremal} relates invariant measures for a skew product and its extension‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}[\cite{crauel1990extremal} and Lemma 2.1 in \cite{homburg2012circle}] \label{L1.1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $F_{(\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$ and consider any invariant measure $\mu^‎+ ‎\in \mathcal{M}_{\nu_\ell^+}(F)$ with disintegration $(\mu^+_{{t}})_{t\in \mathbb{T}^1}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then there is an invariant measure $\mu \in \mathcal{M}_{\nu_\ell}(\mathbb{F}_{F})$ whose disintegration $(\mu_{\textbf{t}})_{\textbf{t} \in \mathcal{S}_\ell}$ is given by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\mu_\textbf{t}=\lim_{n \to \infty}f^n_{t_{-n}}\mu^+_{t_{-n}}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎for $\nu_\ell$-almost every $(\ldots,t_{-1},t_{0})\in \mathcal{S}_\ell$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{The subset $\mathcal{A}_{\ell,\mathbb{T}^1} = \mathcal{A}_\ell (\mathbb{T}^1 \times \mathbb{B}_n)$ of $\mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1 \times \mathbb{B}_n)$}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎we define the subset $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1} $‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{definition}[The sets $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$ and $\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$]\label{ourconditions}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given $\ell \ge 2$‎, ‎denote by $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1} =\mathcal{A}_\ell (\mathbb{T}^1 \times \mathbb{B}_n)$ the subset of $\mathcal{C}_\ell(\mathbb{T}^1 \times \mathbb{B}_n)$ consisting of all skew product maps $F_{(\varphi_\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$ which are contracting on average with respect to the measure $\nu^+_\ell$ and such that there are an open ball $B \subset \mathrm{int}(\mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎disjoint closed intervals $\{L_{1}‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎L_{k}\}$ of $\mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎with $\ell/2 \ge k \ge 3$‎, ‎and a family $\mathcal{F}=\{f_{t_1}‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎f_{t_k}\}$ of fiber maps of $F$ with $t_i\in L_i$ for every $i=1,\dots‎, ‎k$ such that‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item [(A1)] $(B,\mathcal{F})$ is a weak attracting pair‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎\item [(A2)]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎\item [(i)] $f_1\in\mathbb{WC}^2 ( \mathbb{B}_n)$ and there is a normally hyperbolic adapted $C^2$ neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_{f_1}$ of $f_1$ (see Definition \ref{nhyper}) such that $f_t \in \mathcal{N}_{f_1}$ for every $t \in L_1$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">      ‎\item [(ii)] for every $s_i \in L_i$‎, ‎$i=2‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎k$‎, ‎the fiber maps $\{f_{s_2}‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎f_{s_k}\}$ satisfy the covering property‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">    ‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\mathrm{clos}(B)\subset f_{s_2}(B)\cup \cdots \cup f_{s_k}(B);‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\item[(iii)] for every $t\not\in L_1$ the fiber map $f_t$ of $F$ is uniformly contracting‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\item [(A3)] $\varphi_\ell (L_i)=\mathbb{T}^1$ for every $i=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎k$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">     ‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We define the subset $\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell} =\mathcal{A} (\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$ of $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$ as follows‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;"> ‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;"> ‎\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell} \eqdef \big\{\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{F} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n) \,\‎ , ‎\text{such \ that } F \in \mathcal{A}_{\ell‎ ‎,\mathbb{T}^1}\big\}.‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{definition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In Section \ref{assp}‎, ‎we will show that the sets $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$ (and hence the sets $\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$) are not empty for every $\ell$ large enough‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{theorem}\label{thm1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For every $\ell$ sufficiently large‎, ‎there is an open neighbourhood $ \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ of $ \mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ in $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$ such that every $\mathbb{F}\in \mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ has a maximal attractor $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ that is topologically transitive and satisfies the following dichotomy‎:</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\begin{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎\item [$-$] either $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ is an attracting continuous invariant graph;‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎\item [$-$] or $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ is a solenoidal $n$-porcupine-like graph‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">      ‎\end{enumerate}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\end{theorem}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The proof of this theorem is given in Subsection~\ref{complete}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By Lemma \ref{cor0}‎, ‎to be contracting on average is an open condition‎. ‎So‎, ‎fixed $\ell$‎, ‎we can take an open set $\mathcal{W}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1} \supset \mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$ consisting of skew product maps $F_{(\varphi_\ell‎, ‎f_t)}$ which are contracting on average with respect to $\nu^+_\ell$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We let‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{vopen}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell} \eqdef \big\{\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{F} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n) \,\‎ , ‎\text{such \ that } F \in \mathcal{W}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}\big\}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{remark}\label{remopen}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since $\pi_\ell$‎, ‎given by \eqref{e99}‎, ‎is a semiconjugacy between $\varphi_\ell$ and $\xi_\ell$‎, ‎and by Remark \ref{rem90}‎, ‎the fiber maps of $F$ and $\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{F}$ are the same‎, ‎thus‎, ‎$\mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ is open in $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider $\mathbb{F} = \mathbb{F}_{(\xi_\ell‎, ‎\textbf{f}_{\textbf{t}})} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By definition‎, ‎$\mathbb{F}$ is contracting on average with respect to $\nu_\ell$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since $\mathcal{S}_\ell$ and $\mathbb{B}_n$ are compact metric spaces‎, ‎$\xi_\ell$ is a homeomorphism‎, ‎$\nu_\ell$ is an invariant ergodic measure‎, ‎and the fiber maps $f_{\textbf{t}}$ are $C^2$ diffeomorphisms onto their images‎, ‎we can apply Theorem \ref{thmg} to $\mathbb{F}$‎, ‎getting the next result‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proposition}\label{p.thm000}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\mathbb{F}\in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then there exist a $\xi_\ell$-invariant set $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}_\ell\subseteq \mathcal{S}_\ell$ with $\nu_\ell(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}_\ell )=1$ and a measurable function $\gamma_{\mathbb{F}} \colon \widetilde{\mathcal{S }}_\ell\to \mathbb{B}_n$ whose graph is invariant and attracting under $\mathbb{F}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proposition}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{Skew products with high dimensional porcupine graph}\label{complete}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By shrinking the set $\mathcal{W}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$‎, ‎we may assume that every fiber map $f_t$ of any $F \in \mathcal{W}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$ belongs to $\text{Inj}^2(\mathbb{B}_n)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By Remark \ref{rem90}‎, ‎the same holds for any $\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{F} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This implies that $\mathcal{S}_\ell \times \mathbb{B}_n$ is a trapping region of $\mathbb{F}$‎, ‎hence its maximal attractor‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{e67}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎A_{max}(\mathbb{F})\eqdef \bigcap_{n\geq 0}\mathbb{F}^n(\mathcal{S_\ell} \times \mathbb{B}_n)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is well defined‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎the integer $\ell$ remains fixed and hence the subscript $\ell$ will be omitted‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm1}]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take $\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{F} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By Proposition~\ref{p.thm000}‎, ‎there are a $\xi$-invariant set $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}\subset \mathcal{S}$ with $\nu(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}) = 1$ and a measurable function $\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}‎ : ‎\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}\to \mathbb{B}_n$ whose graph $\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$‎, ‎denoted by $\Gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$‎, ‎is $\mathbb{F}$-invariant‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{inclusion}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{F} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then $\Gamma_{\mathbb{F}} \subset A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$‎. ‎Moreover‎, ‎if $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}=\mathcal{S}$ then $\Gamma_{\mathbb{F}}= A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For each $\textbf{t}=(\ldots‎, ‎t_{-n}\ldots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0)\in \mathcal{S}$ define‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{nested}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\mathbb{B}^k(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})\eqdef f_{\xi_\ell^{-1}(\textbf{t})}\circ \dots \circ f_{\xi_\ell^{-k}(\textbf{t})}(\mathbb{B}_n)=f_{t_{-1}}\circ \dots \circ f_{t_{-k}}(\mathbb{B}_n)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎As the fiber maps $f_{t_{-j}}$ belong to $\text{Inj}^2(\mathbb{B}_n)$ the sets $\mathbb{B}^k(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ are well defined and form a nested sequence of compact sets‎. ‎Thus‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\begin{equation}\label{nonempty}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})\eqdef \bigcap_{k=0}^\infty \mathbb{B}^k(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})\neq \emptyset‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{aa}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎A_{max}(\mathbb{F}) \bigcap (\{\textbf{t}\} \times \mathbb{B}_{n}) = \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that‎, ‎$\Gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$ is attracting‎, ‎so‎, ‎for each $\textbf{t} \in \widetilde{\mathcal{S}}$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\lim f_{\xi_\ell^{-1}(\textbf{t})}\circ \dots \circ f_{\xi_\ell^{-n}(\textbf{t})}(\mathbb{B}_n)=\lim f_{\xi_\ell^{-1}(\textbf{t})}\circ \dots \circ f_{\xi_\ell^{-n}(\textbf{t})}( \gamma_\mathbb{F}(\textbf{t}))= B^\infty(\textbf{t}‎ , ‎\mathbb{F})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since the graph $\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$ is invariant‎, ‎we get‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\Gamma_\mathbb{F} = \bigcup_{\textbf{t} \in \widetilde{\mathcal{S}}} B^\infty(\textbf{t}‎ , ‎\mathbb{F}) \subset A_{max}(\mathbb{F})‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎proving the first part of the lemma‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For the second part‎, ‎assuming that $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}= \mathcal{S}$‎, ‎note that by \eqref{aa} it holds‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎A_{max}(\mathbb{F}) = \bigcup_{\textbf{t} \in {\mathcal{S}}} \Big( A_{max}(\mathbb{F}) \cap \big( \{\textbf{t}\} \times \mathbb{B}_n \big) \Big) = \bigcup_{\textbf{t} \in {\mathcal{S}}} \{\textbf{t}\} \times \mathbb{B}^\infty (\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})= \Gamma_\mathbb{F}‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎which ends the proof of the lemma‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By Lemma~\ref{inclusion}‎, ‎if $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}=\mathcal{S}$ then $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ is an invariant graph and the graph function $\gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$ is defined on the whole space $\mathcal{S}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this case‎, ‎we have $\Gamma_{\mathbb{F}} = A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For the case that $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}\neq\mathcal{S}$‎, ‎the lemma below states the structure of non-trivial spines‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{tangent}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\mathbb{F} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ and there exist $\textbf{t} \in \mathcal{S} \setminus \widetilde{\mathcal{S}}$ for that $ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is nontrivial‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then the set $ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is a one dimensional submanifold tangent to the central bundle‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{F}_{F} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell} $ with nontrivial spine $ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$‎, ‎for $\textbf{t} \in \mathcal{S} \setminus \widetilde{\mathcal{S}}$‎. ‎Then $F \in \mathcal{W}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1} $‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By item $(iii)$ of Condition ($A2$) of Definition \ref{ourconditions} and shrinking $\mathcal{W}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1} $‎, ‎for each $t \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1$‎, ‎the fiber maps $f_t$ of $F$ are uniformly contracting‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By Remark \ref{rem90}‎, ‎the fiber maps of $\mathbb{F}_{F}$ and $F$ are the same‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎So‎, ‎this fact holds for every $\mathbb{F} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell} $ and every $\textbf{t}=(\dots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0) \in \mathcal{S} $ with $t_0 \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1 $‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Given $\textbf{t}=(\dots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0) \in \mathcal{S} $‎, ‎define‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">    ‎n(\textbf{t})\eqdef \sup \{j‎: ‎t_{-j} \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1\}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">   ‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎If $n(\textbf{t})$ is finite‎, ‎then for each $j &gt; n(\textbf{t})$‎, ‎$t_{-j}\in L_1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Therefore‎, ‎By item $(i)$ of Condition ($A2$) of Definition \ref{ourconditions} and Proposition \ref{open}‎, ‎the intersection $\bigcap_{m\geq 0} \mathbb{B}^m(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})=\lim_{n \to \infty}f_{t_{-1}}\circ \dots \circ f_{t_{-m}}(\mathbb{B}_n)$ is either a single point or a one dimensional $C^1$ submanifold‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎The hypothesis that $ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is nontrivial‎, ‎prevents the first case‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎So‎, ‎$ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is a one dimensional $C^1$ submanifold‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Now‎, ‎assume that $n(\textbf{t})$ is infinite‎. ‎If the set $\{j‎: ‎t_{-j} \in L_1\}$ is finite‎, ‎a straightforward argument shows that $ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is a single point that contradicts the hypothesis‎. ‎So‎, ‎the set $\{j‎: ‎t_{-j} \in L_1\}$ is infinite‎. ‎This means that there are infinitely many $t_{-j} \in L_1$ and also infinitely many $t_{-j} \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1$‎. ‎In this case‎, ‎the proof follows from Theorem \ref{open2} and the hypothesis that $ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is nontrivial‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎Note that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎A_{max}(\mathbb{F})=\bigcup_{\textbf{t} \in \mathcal{S}}\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{corspine}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\mathbb{F} \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ and suppose that there exists a nontrivial spine $ \mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$‎, ‎for some $\textbf{t} \in \mathcal{S}$‎. ‎Then the set of nontrivial spines has the cardinality of the continuum and it is dense in $\mathcal{S}$‎. ‎Furthermore‎, ‎the same holds for the set $\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For $t_j \in \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎$j=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎m$‎, ‎we denote by $\bar t(m)=(t_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎t_m)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Define‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$\mathrm{dist} (\bar t(m)‎ , ‎\bar t'(m))\eqdef \max \{d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_1,t^{\prime}_1)‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_m,t^{\prime}_m) \},$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where $\bar t(m)=(t_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎t_m)$ and $\bar t^{\prime}(m)=(t^{\prime}_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_m)$‎. ‎Also‎, ‎given $t_j \in \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎$j=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎m$‎, ‎denote by $f_{[\bar t(m)]}\eqdef f_{[t_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎t_m]}\eqdef f_{t_1} \circ \dots \circ f_{t_m}$‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{claim}\label{c2121}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎For every $m \geq 1$ and $\varepsilon &gt; 0$ there exists $\delta =\delta(m‎, ‎\varepsilon)&gt; 0$ such that for every $t_j‎, ‎t^{\prime}_j \in \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎$j=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎m$‎, ‎with‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\mathrm{dist} (\bar t(m)‎ , ‎\bar t'(m)) &lt; \delta‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎then‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎|\text{diam}(f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))‎ - ‎\text{diam}(f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))|&lt; \varepsilon‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{claim}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fix any $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\varepsilon&gt;0$‎. ‎Since the map $(\bar t(m),y)\mapsto f_{[\bar t(m)]}(y)$ is uniformly continuous‎, ‎thus‎, ‎there exists $\delta &gt; 0$ such that for each $t_j‎, ‎t^{\prime}_j \in \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎$j=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎m$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$ \max \{\mathrm{dist} (\bar t(m)‎ , ‎\bar t'(m)),d_{\mathbb{B}_n}(x,y) \} &lt; \delta‎, ‎\quad \text{implies \ that } \quad d_{\mathbb{B}_n}(f_{[\bar t(m)]}(x)‎ , ‎f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(y))&lt;\varepsilon/2.$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take $\bar t(m)= (t_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎t_m)$ and $\bar t'(m)=(t^{\prime}_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_m)$ in $\mathbb{T}^m$ such that $ \mathrm{dist} (\bar t(m)‎ , ‎\bar t'(m)) &lt; \delta$‎. ‎For $A \subseteq \mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎let $U_{\varepsilon‎ /‎2}(A) \eqdef \{y \in \mathbb{B}_n‎ : ‎d_{\mathbb{B}_n}(y,A)\leq \varepsilon‎ /2\}$.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We need to show that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{inc}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset U_{\varepsilon/2}(f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)) \quad \text{and} \quad f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset U_{\varepsilon/2}(f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This implies that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$\text{diam}(f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))\leq \text{diam}(f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))‎ + ‎\varepsilon \quad \text{and} \quad \text{diam}(f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))\leq \text{diam}(f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))‎ + ‎\varepsilon,$$ proving the claim‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎We now prove \eqref{inc}‎. ‎Given $y \in f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)$ we can write $y=f_{[\bar t(m)]}(x)$ for some $x \in \mathbb{B}_n$‎. ‎Hence‎, ‎taking $y^{\prime}=f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(x)$‎, ‎we have‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎d_{\mathbb{B}_n}(y,y^{\prime})=d_{\mathbb{B}_n}(f_{[\bar t(m)]}(x),f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(x))&lt;\varepsilon/2‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎proving that $y \in U_{\varepsilon/2}(f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))$‎. ‎The proof of the other inclusion of (\ref{inc}) is similar‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In what follows‎, ‎for each $\textbf{t} = (\ldots‎ , ‎t_{-m},\ldots‎ , ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_{0}) \in \mathcal{S} $ the finite word $\tilde{t}(m) = (t_{-1},\ldots‎ , ‎t_{-m})$ is called the corresponding $m$-word of $\textbf{t}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let us take the disjoint closed intervals $I_i \subset \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎$i=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎\ell$‎, ‎with length $1‎/ ‎\ell$‎, ‎such that $\mathbb{T}^1=\bigcup_{i=1}^\ell I_i$ and there exist indices $i_j$‎, ‎$j=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎k$‎, ‎so that $I_{i_j}=L_j$‎, ‎where $L_j$ are disjoint closed intervals of $\mathbb{T}^1$ given by Definition \ref{ourconditions}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then by the choice of $I_{i}$‎ , ‎$\varphi_\ell (I_i)=\mathbb{T}^1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fix any $\textbf{t}=(\dots‎, ‎t_{-1}‎, ‎t_0) \in \mathcal{S}$ for that $\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is a non-trivial spine‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take $\varepsilon &gt; 0$ with‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{m2}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\varepsilon &lt;\frac{1}{8} \text{diam}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F}))‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎and $m$ sufficiently large such that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{m3}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎U_{\varepsilon/2}( f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))\subset U_{\varepsilon}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F}))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎where $\tilde{t}(m) = (t_{-1}‎, ‎\ldots‎ , ‎t_{-m})$ is the corresponding $m$-word of $\textbf{t}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By Claim \ref{c2121} and inclusions (\ref{inc})‎, ‎for $\varepsilon$ given by (\ref{m2})‎ , ‎there exists $\delta &gt; 0$ such that for each $\textbf{t}^{\prime}=(\ldots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-m}‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-1}‎, ‎t^{\prime}_0) \in \mathcal{S}$ and the corresponding $m$-word $\bar t^{\prime}(m)=(t^{\prime}_{-1}‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-m})$ of $\textbf{t}^{\prime}$‎ , ‎if $ \max\{d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_0‎, ‎t^{\prime}_0)‎, ‎{dist} (\bar t(m)‎ , ‎\bar t'(m))\} &lt; \delta$ then‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{m1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset U_{\varepsilon/2}(f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))‎, ‎\quad f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset U_{\varepsilon/2}(f_{[\bar t(m)]})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Now‎, ‎we choose a sequence $\textbf{t}^{\prime}=(\ldots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-n}\ldots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-1}‎, ‎t^{\prime}_0)\in \mathcal{S}$ with $d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_{0}‎ , ‎t^{\prime}_{0})&lt;\delta$‎, ‎so that for each $j$‎, ‎$t_{-j}$ and $t^{\prime}_{-j}$ belong to the same closed interval $I_{i_j}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since $\varphi_\ell(t_{-j-1})=t_{-j}$ and $\varphi_\ell(t^{\prime}_{-j-1})=t^{\prime}_{-j}$‎, ‎hence $t_{-j-1}‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-j-1} \in \varphi^{-1}_\ell(I_j)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that the inverse branch $\varphi^{-1}_\ell|_{I_j}$ is a contraction with a uniform contracting rate $1/\ell$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎for each $j$‎, ‎$d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_{-j-1}‎ , ‎t^{\prime}_{-j-1})&lt; 1/\ell d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_{-j}‎ , ‎t^{\prime}_{-j})$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎for each $j$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{dd1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_{-j}‎ , ‎t^{\prime}_{-j})&lt;\delta‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎which implies that $\max\{d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_0‎, ‎t^{\prime}_0)‎, ‎{dist} (\bar t(m)‎ , ‎\bar t'(m))\} &lt; \delta.$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then‎, ‎by (\ref{m3})‎ , ‎(\ref{m1}) and (\ref{dd1})‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{m30}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎U_{\varepsilon/2}( f_{[\bar t^\prime(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n))\subset U_{\varepsilon}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t}^\prime,\mathbb{F}))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Therefore‎, ‎by (\ref{m1}) and (\ref{m30})‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{m5}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F}) \subset f_{[\bar t(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)\subset U_{\varepsilon/2}(f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)) \subset U_{\varepsilon}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t}^{\prime},\mathbb{F}))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consequently‎, ‎by \eqref{m5} and \eqref{m2}‎, ‎we get‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{prime}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">  ‎\text{diam}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F}))\leq \text{diam}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t}^{\prime},\mathbb{F}))+2\varepsilon \leq \text{diam}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t}^{\prime},\mathbb{F}))‎ + ‎\frac{1}{4}\text{diam}(\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F}))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By (\ref{prime})‎, ‎since $\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t},\mathbb{F})$ is a non-trivial spine‎, ‎$\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{t}^{\prime},\mathbb{F})$ is also a nontrivial spine‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎As a result‎, ‎the set of nontrivial spines has the cardinality of the continuum but it is not an open subset of $\mathcal{S}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Because‎, ‎for each $t_0^{\prime}\in \mathbb{T}^1$ with $d_{\mathbb{T}^1}(t_{0}‎ , ‎t^{\prime}_{0})&lt;\delta$‎ , ‎there exists only one inverse limit branch $\textbf{t}^{\prime}=(\ldots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-m}\ldots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-1}‎, ‎t^{\prime}_0)$ that fulfills the construction described above‎, ‎namely‎, ‎for each $j$‎, ‎$t_{-j}$ and $t^{\prime}_{-j}$ belong to the same closed interval $I_{i_{j}}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎To prove the next statement‎, ‎take any $\textbf{t}^{\prime}$ with a nontrivial spine as above such that $t^{\prime}_{0}$ is irrational and let $\textbf{s}_k=\xi_\ell^k(\textbf{t}^{\prime})=(\dots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_{-j}‎, ‎\ldots‎, ‎t^{\prime}_0‎, ‎\varphi_\ell(t^{\prime}_0)‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎\varphi_\ell^k(t^{\prime}_0))$‎, ‎for each positive integer $k$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then‎, ‎by continuity‎, ‎for each $k\geq 1$‎, ‎we have‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$\lim_{m \to \infty}f_{[\bar s_k(k+m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)=\lim_{m \to \infty} f_{\varphi_\ell^k(t^{\prime}_0)}\circ \dots \circ f_{t^{\prime}_0}\circ \dots \circ f_{t^{\prime}_{-m}}(\mathbb{B}_n)= f_{\varphi_\ell^k(t^{\prime}_0)}\circ \dots \circ f_{\varphi_\ell(t^{\prime}_0)} (\lim_{m \to \infty} f_{[\bar t^{\prime}(m)]}(\mathbb{B}_n)).$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consequently‎, ‎$\mathbb{B}^{\infty}(\textbf{s}_k,\mathbb{F})$ is a non-trivial spine‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since $t_0^{\prime}$ is irrational‎, ‎the $\varphi_\ell$-orbit of $t_0^{\prime}$ is dense in $\mathbb{T}^1$‎. ‎Since the base maps $\varphi_\ell$ and $\xi_\ell$ are semiconjugate‎, ‎the $\xi_\ell$-orbit of $\textbf{t}^{\prime}$ is also dense in $\mathcal{S}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎the set of nontrivial spines is dense in $\mathcal{S}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎On the other hand‎, ‎by Proposition \ref{p.thm000}‎, ‎$\nu_\ell(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}})=1$‎. ‎Also‎, ‎$\nu_\ell$ inherited from $\nu_\ell^+$ and $\nu_\ell^+$ is equivalent to Lebesgue‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By these facts‎, ‎$\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}$ has the cardinality of the continuum and it is dense in $\mathcal{S}$‎. ‎This finishes the proof‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By Lemma~\ref{inclusion}‎, ‎$\Gamma_{\mathbb{F}} \subset A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$‎, ‎and hence the closure of $\Gamma_{\mathbb{F}}$ is contained in $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$‎. ‎The next lemma provides the inclusion in the another direction‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{geometry-short}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎If $\mathbb F\in\mathcal W_{\mathcal S_\ell}$ has invariant graph $\Gamma_{\mathbb F}$ and $\mathcal S\setminus\widetilde{\mathcal S}\neq\varnothing$‎, ‎then every non-trivial spine is contained in $\overline{\Gamma_{\mathbb F}}$ and‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\[‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\overline{\Gamma_{\mathbb F}}=A_{\max}(\mathbb F)‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\]‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎$\pi_2(A_{\max}(\mathbb F))$ has nonempty interior‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Recall two facts used below‎: ‎(i) by Proposition~\ref{p.thm000} there is an attracting invariant graph $\Gamma_{\mathbb F}\subset A_{\max}(\mathbb F)$ defined on $\widetilde{\mathcal S}$; (ii) by the covering property in Definition~\ref{ourconditions} there exists an open ball $B\subset\operatorname{int}\mathbb B_n$ whose images under appropriate fiber maps cover $\overline B$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Fix an arbitrary $(t,x)\in A_{\max}(\mathbb F)$ and a neighbourhood $U\times V$ of $(t,x)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since $\varphi_\ell:\mathbb T^1\to\mathbb T^1$‎, ‎$\varphi_\ell(\theta)=\ell\theta\pmod1$‎, ‎is topologically transitive (indeed mixing)‎, ‎its natural extension (the inverse limit) $(\mathcal S_\ell,\xi_\ell)$ is topologically transitive and therefore admits points with dense $\xi_\ell$--orbit‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Because $\widetilde{\mathcal S}$ contains a dense set of admissible sequences (for instance those with irrational coordinates used in the construction)‎, ‎we may choose $s\in\widetilde{\mathcal S}$ whose $\xi_\ell$--orbit is dense in $\mathcal S_\ell$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By (ii) pick $s_0\in\widetilde{\mathcal S}$ with $\gamma_{\mathbb F}(s_0)\in B$‎. ‎The density of the $\xi_\ell$--orbit of $s$ implies that some iterate $\xi_\ell^n(s)$ lies arbitrarily close to $t$ (hence in $U$ for large $n$)‎. ‎Let $w=(t_{-n},\dots,t_0)$ denote the corresponding finite word and set $h:=f_{t_{-n}}^{-1}\circ\cdots\circ f_{t_0}^{-1}$‎. ‎By continuity of finite compositions and openness of $B$‎, ‎for sufficiently small $V$ we have $h(V)\cap B\neq\varnothing$‎. ‎Choose $x'\in h(V)\cap B$; by Proposition~\ref{p.thm000}‎, ‎there exists $s'\in\widetilde{\mathcal S}$ with $\gamma_{\mathbb F}(s')=x'$‎. ‎Concatenating the finite word $w$ to $s'$ produces a sequence whose forward image lies in $U\times V$‎, ‎hence $(U\times V)\cap\Gamma_{\mathbb F}\neq\varnothing$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since $(t,x)\in A_{\max}(\mathbb F)$ and $U\times V$ was arbitrary‎, ‎every neighbourhood of $(t,x)$ meets $\Gamma_{\mathbb F}$; therefore $A_{\max}(\mathbb F)\subset\overline{\Gamma_{\mathbb F}}$‎. ‎Together with the inclusion $\Gamma_{\mathbb F}\subset A_{\max}(\mathbb F)$ this yields $\overline{\Gamma_{\mathbb F}}=A_{\max}(\mathbb F)$‎. ‎Every non-trivial spine is contained in $A_{\max}(\mathbb F)$‎, ‎hence in $\overline{\Gamma_{\mathbb F}}$‎. ‎Finally‎, ‎by (ii) the projection $\pi_2(A_{\max}(\mathbb F))$ contains $B$ and thus has nonempty interior‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Now‎, ‎we show that the maximal attractor is transitive‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let us recall the definition of a solenoid map (\cite{williams1974expanding})‎. ‎Let $R \geq 2$ and $\alpha \ll 1$ and take‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$D:=\{z \in \mathbb{C}‎ : ‎|z| \leq R\}‎, ‎\ N:=\mathbb{T}^1 \times D‎. ‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The solenoid map $T‎ : ‎N \to N$ is defined by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$T‎ : ‎(t,z) \mapsto (\varphi_\ell(t)‎, ‎e^{2\pi i t}‎+ ‎\alpha z)‎, ‎\ t\in \mathbb{T}^1‎, ‎\ z\in D.$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎The maximal attractor of $T$‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$\Delta_T:=\bigcap_{n\geq 0}T^n(N),$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎is called a Smale-Williams solenoid‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{remark}\label{tra}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By \cite[Theorem~7.1]{robinson1998dynamical} $\Delta_T$ is invariant‎, ‎locally maximal‎, ‎and hyperbolic‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Moreover‎, ‎the restriction $T|_{\Delta_T}$ is invertible and topologically transitive \cite[Proposition~7.5]{robinson1998dynamical}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By \cite[Theorem~7.7]{robinson1998dynamical}‎, ‎the solenoid map $T|_{\Delta_T}$ and the extension map $\xi_\ell$ are conjugated‎, ‎therefore $\xi_\ell$ is also topologically transitive‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{remark}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{milnor}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The maximal attractor $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ is a transitive set‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\gamma_\mathbb{F}$ be the graph function of $\mathbb{F}$‎. ‎Proposition \ref{p.thm000} ensures the existence of $\gamma_\mathbb{F}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take $\Gamma_\mathbb{F}$ the graph of $\gamma_\mathbb{F}$ and consider a point $(\textbf{t}^{\prime}‎ , ‎\gamma(\textbf{t}^{\prime}))\in \Gamma_\mathbb{F}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then $\bigcap_{m\geq 0}f_{\textbf{t}^{\prime}_{-1}}\circ \dots \circ f_{\textbf{t}^{\prime}_{-m}}(\mathbb{B}_n)= \{\gamma_\mathbb{F}(\textbf{t}^{\prime})\}$ and‎, ‎in particular‎, ‎for each $x \in \mathbb{B}_n$‎, ‎one has $\gamma_\mathbb{F}(\textbf{t}^{\prime})=\lim_{m \to \infty}f_{\textbf{t}^{\prime}_{-1}}\circ \dots \circ f_{\textbf{t}^{\prime}_{-m}}(x)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎This fact implies that a point $(\textbf{t}^{\prime}‎ , ‎\gamma_\mathbb{F}(\textbf{t}^{\prime}))\in \Gamma_\mathbb{F}$ belongs to the $\omega$-limit set of a point $(\textbf{t}‎ , ‎x)$ when the sequence $\textbf{t}^{\prime}$ belongs to the $\omega$-limit set of $\textbf{t}$‎. ‎Since $\xi_\ell$ is topologically transitive (see Remark \ref{tra})‎, ‎so it has a dense orbit‎. ‎Let the orbit of $\textbf{t}$ be dense‎. ‎Thus‎, ‎each sequence $\textbf{t}^{\prime} \in \mathcal{S}$ belongs to the $\omega$-limit set of $\textbf{t}$‎. ‎By this fact the graph $\Gamma_\mathbb{F}$ is contained in the limit set of $\mathbb{F}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since the limit set of $\mathbb{F}$ is closed‎, ‎hence the closure of $\Gamma_\mathbb{F}$ is contained in the limit set‎. ‎By Lemma \ref{geometry-short}‎, ‎the closure of $\Gamma_\mathbb{F}$ is equal to $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Therefore‎, ‎$\omega(\textbf{t}‎ , ‎x)=A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ which implies that $A_{max}(\mathbb{F})$ is topologically transitive‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎To prove Theorem \ref{thm1}‎, ‎it is enough to take‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{ww}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎\mathcal{V}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell} \eqdef \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The proof is completed‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\subsection{The sets $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$ and $\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ are nonempty}\label{assp}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎In this subsection we construct skew products satisfying Conditions ($A1$)-($A3$) of Definition \ref{ourconditions} which imply that the sets $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$ and $\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ are nonempty‎.</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{theorem}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The sets $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1}$ and $\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}$ are nonempty‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{theorem}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consider an open set $B$ and a finite family $\mathcal{F}=\{f_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎f_k\}$ of $C^2$ diffeomorphisms defined on $\mathbb{B}_n$ such that $(\mathcal{F},B)$ is a weakly attracting pair and $B$ is a weak blending region for $\mathcal{F}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎Theorem \ref{problend} ensures the existence of this weakly attracting pair $(\mathcal{F},B)$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By the proof of Theorem \ref{problend}‎, ‎$f_1 \in \mathbb{W}\mathbb{C}^2(\mathbb{B}_n)$ is a weakly contractive map and $f_i \in \text{Inj}^2(\mathbb{B}_n)$‎, ‎$i=2‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎k$‎, ‎are defined by $f_i=A_i \circ g \circ A_i^{-1}$‎, ‎where $A_i$ are linear isometries which fixes 0 and $g$ is a uniformly contracting map‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Take a large integer $\ell‎ /‎2 \geq k$‎, ‎the expanding map $\varphi_\ell(t)=\ell t \ (mod 1)$ and disjoint closed intervals $\{ L_1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎L_k\}$ of $\mathbb{T}^1$ with $\varphi_\ell(L_i)=\mathbb{T}^1$‎. ‎Take points $t_i \in L_i$ and let $f_{t_i}=f_i$ for $i=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎k$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Assume $t_1=0$ and consider a $C^\infty$ map $\eta‎ : ‎\mathbb{T}^1 \to [0‎, ‎1]$ such that $\eta (0)=0$ and $\eta(t)\equiv1$‎, ‎for all $t \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let $\text{Iso}_0$ be the space of all of linear isometries of $\mathbb{R}^n$ that fixes 0 and take a continuous map $A‎ : ‎\mathbb{T}^1 \to \text{Iso}_0$ with $A(t_i)=A_i$‎, ‎for all $2 \leq i \leq k$‎, ‎such that $A(t) \circ g \circ A(t)^{-1}$ maps $\mathbb{B}_n$ into itself‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Define‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{map1}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"> ‎f_t:=(1‎- ‎\eta(t)^2)f_1‎ + ‎\eta(t)^2( A(t) \circ g \circ A(t)^{-1})‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Hence‎, ‎$t \mapsto f_t$‎, ‎$t \in \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎is an arc of $C^2$ maps‎, ‎where $f_t$ is given by (\ref{map1})‎. ‎By definition of $f_t$‎, ‎$f_{t_i}=f_i$‎, ‎for each $1\leq i \leq k$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Also‎, ‎by a straightforward computations‎, ‎it is easily seen that‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation}\label{contract}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\forall t \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1 \ \text{and} \ \forall x \in \mathbb{B}_n‎, ‎\ \text{one \ has} \ \|Df_t(x)\| &lt; 1‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Indeed‎, ‎for each $t \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1$‎, ‎$\eta(t)=1$ and hence‎, ‎$f_t=\eta(t)^2( A(t) \circ g \circ A(t)^{-1})$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Since $g$ is uniformly contracting‎, ‎$A(t) \in \text{Iso}_0$ and by \cite[Theorem~5.3]{conrad2010isometries}‎, ‎we get $\|Df_t(x)\| \leq \|Dg(x)\|&lt; 1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Now we define a skew product $F \colon \mathbb{T}^1 \times \mathbb{B}_n \to \mathbb{T}^1 \times \mathbb{B}_n$ over the linear expanding circle map $\varphi_\ell(t)=\ell t \ (\text{mod} \ 1)$ and with the fiber maps $f_t$‎, ‎$t \in \mathbb{T}^1$‎, ‎as defined in (\ref{map1})‎, ‎by‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$ (t,x)\mapsto (\varphi_\ell(t)‎, ‎f_t(x))‎. ‎$$‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{lemma}\label{lemma0}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The skew product $F$ is contracting on average‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{lemma}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{proof}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎The map $(t,x)\mapsto f_t(x)$ is continuous in both variables $t\in \mathbb{T}^1‎, ‎x \in \mathbb{B}_n$ and it is $C^2$ in $x$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Hence‎, ‎there exists $\varsigma \in L^1(\mathbb{T}^1,\nu_\ell^+)$ such that $d(f_t(x),f_t(x^{\prime}))&lt;\varsigma(t)d(x,x^{\prime})$‎, ‎for $\nu_\ell^+$-almost every $t$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Let‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$ \Delta(\mathbb{B}_n)=\{(x,x^{\prime}) \in \mathbb{B}_n \times \mathbb{B}_n \colon x\neq x^{\prime}\}.$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Then‎, ‎by \cite[Lem.~5.1]{stark1999regularity}‎, ‎for every $n\geq 0$ the number‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\varsigma_n(t)‎ : ‎=\sup_{(x,x^{\prime})\in \Delta(\mathbb{B}_n)}\frac{d(f_t^n(x)‎ , ‎f_t^n(x^{\prime}))}{d(x,x^{\prime})}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎exists for almost every $t\in \mathbb{T}^1$ and the map $\varsigma_n$ is measurable‎, ‎where $f_{\varphi_\ell^n(t)}\circ \dots \circ f_{\varphi_\ell(t)} \circ f_{t}$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Also‎, ‎by \cite[Lem.~5.3]{stark1999regularity}‎, ‎the sequence $\varsigma_n$ is submultiplicative and hence $\log \varsigma_n$ is subadditive‎, ‎that is‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:2;">‎\log \varsigma_{n+m}(t)\leq \log \varsigma_n(t)‎+ ‎\log \varsigma_m(\xi^n(t))‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎$$‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Applying Kingman's subadditive ergodic theorem \cite[Thm.~5.4]{krengel2011ergodic} to $\log \varsigma_n$‎, ‎we obtain that the map‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\lambda(t)=\lim_{n \to‎ +‎\infty}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_n(t)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎exists for $\nu_\ell^+$-almost every $t$ and is $\varphi_\ell$ invariant‎. ‎Moreover‎, ‎by subadditivity‎,</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\begin{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\lim_{n \to \infty}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_n(t)= \inf_{n}\frac{1}{n}\log \varsigma_n(t)‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{equation*}‎</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎and‎, ‎by the ergodicity of $\nu_\ell^+$‎, ‎it is constant $\nu_\ell^+$-a.e.‎, ‎say equal to $\lambda$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that if $t$ is irrational‎, ‎the $\varphi_\ell$-orbit of $t$ is dense in $\mathbb{T}^1$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Consequently‎, ‎there exists a sequence $n_j$ such that $\varphi_\ell^{n_j}(t) \in \mathbb{T}^1 \setminus L_1$‎, ‎and hence‎, ‎$\|Df_{\varphi_\ell^{n_j}(t)}\| &lt; 1$‎. ‎This implies that $\log \varsigma_n(t)&lt;0$ and therefore‎, ‎$\lambda(t)&lt;0$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Note that the subset of irrational numbers has full Lebesgue measure‎. ‎By Remark \ref{circ-measure}‎, ‎$\nu^+_\ell$ is equivalent to Lebesgue and hence‎, ‎$\nu^+_\ell$ almost all $t\in \mathbb{T}^1$ has a dense $\varphi_\ell$-orbit‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By these facts for $\nu_\ell^+$-almost all $t$‎, ‎$\lambda(t)&lt;0$‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By the ergodicity of $\nu_\ell^+$‎, ‎$\lambda(t)&lt;0$ is constant $\nu_\ell^+$-a.e‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎the skew product $F$ is a contracting on average skew product‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎By shrinking the closed intervals $L_i$‎, ‎$i=1‎, ‎\dots‎, ‎k$‎, ‎and by Lemma \ref{lemma0}‎, ‎all Conditions of Definition \ref{ourconditions} hold for $F$‎, ‎thus‎, ‎the family $\mathcal{A}_{\ell‎, ‎\mathbb{T}^1} $ is nonempty‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎Thus‎, ‎$\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{S}_\ell} $ is also nonempty‎.</p>
<p style=" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;">‎\end{proof}‎</p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
<p style="-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; -qt-user-state:0;"></p>
